{{Saudi-led intervention in the Yemeni civil war infobox}}
{{Campaignbox Yemeni Crisis}}
{{Yemeni Crisis (2011–present)}}
On 26 March 2015, [[Saudi Arabia]], leading a coalition of nine countries from West Asia and North Africa, staged a military intervention in [[Yemen]] at the request of Yemeni president [[Abdrabbuh Mansur Hadi]], who had been ousted from the capital, [[Sanaa]], in September 2014 by [[Houthi insurgency|Houthi insurgents]] during the [[Yemeni civil war (2014–present)|Yemeni civil war]]. Efforts by the [[United Nations]] (UN) to facilitate a power sharing arrangement under a new transitional government collapsed, leading to escalating conflict between government forces, Houthi rebels, and other armed groups, which culminated in Hadi fleeing to Saudi Arabia shortly before it began military operations in the country.

The first month of the intervention, codenamed '''Operation Decisive Storm''' ({{langx|ar|عملية عاصفة الحزم|Amaliyyat 'Āṣifat al-Ḥazm}}), consisted of airstrikes on Houthi rebels and a [[blockade of Yemen|full blockade]].<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Roberts |first1=Dan |last2=Shaheen |first2=Kareem |date=2015-03-26 |title=Saudi Arabia launches Yemen air strikes as alliance builds against Houthi rebels |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2015/mar/26/saudi-arabia-begins-airstrikes-against-houthi-in-yemen |access-date=2024-04-01 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> On 22 April, the Saudi-led coalition declared that it had achieved its initial goals and announced '''Operation Restoring Hope''', which would comprise a "combination of political, diplomatic and military action" while continuing "to prevent the Houthi militias from moving or undertaking any operations inside Yemen".<ref>[https://www.reuters.com/article/idUSKBN0NC24S/ Saudi-led coalition announces end to Yemen operation] ''Reuters'' (April 2015)</ref> Ground forces were subsequently deployed into the country<ref>Gatten, Emma. [https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/middle-east/saudi-blockade-starves-yemen-of-vital-supplies-as-bombing-raids-continue-10509460.html "Saudi blockade starves Yemen of vital supplies, as bombing raids continue"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151002223144/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/middle-east/saudi-blockade-starves-yemen-of-vital-supplies-as-bombing-raids-continue-10509460.html |date=2 October 2015 }}. ''[[The Independent]]''. 19 September 2016. Retrieved 23 October 2016.</ref> as part of a broader offensive against both [[Houthi]] militants and loyalists of Hadi's predecessor, [[Ali Abdullah Saleh]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Gulf Coalition Operations in Yemen (Part 1): The Ground War |publisher=The Washington Institute |url=https://www.washingtoninstitute.org/policy-analysis/gulf-coalition-operations-yemen-part-1-ground-war |access-date=2024-04-02 |language=en |archive-date=6 December 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171206195626/http://www.washingtoninstitute.org/policy-analysis/view/gulf-coalition-operations-in-yemen-part-1-the-ground-war |url-status=live }}</ref> Owing to Iran's support of these factions, the conflict is widely regarded as part of the broader [[Iran–Saudi Arabia proxy conflict|Saudi-Iran proxy conflict]].

[[Egypt]], [[Morocco]], [[Jordan]], [[Sudan]], and the [[United Arab Emirates]] provided air and ground forces, while [[Kuwait]], [[Qatar]], [[Bahrain]], and U.S. private military contractor [[Constellis]] headed several ground operations. [[Djibouti]], [[Eritrea]], and [[Somalia]] opened their airspace, territorial waters, and military bases to coalition forces.<ref name="Ssfpstsciy">{{cite news |title=SOMALIA: Somalia finally pledges support to Saudi-led coalition in Yemen |work=wargeyska |url=http://www.wargeyska.so/somalia-somalia-finally-pledges-support-to-suadi-led-coalition-in-yemen-raxanreeb-online/ |agency=RBC Radio |date=7 April 2015 |access-date=7 April 2015}}{{dead link|date=July 2026}}</ref> At varying stages, the intervention was backed by the United States, United Kingdom, France, Germany, and Canada. The U.S. provided intelligence and logistical support, such as [[aerial refueling]] and search-and-rescue for downed coalition pilots,<ref name="hp-20160810">{{cite news |url=https://huffingtonpost.com/entry/barack-obama-could-end-the-killing-in-yemen-in-hours_us_57f7d5dee4b0b6a43031ba4e |title=Obama Could End the Slaughter in Yemen Within Hours |author=Akbar Shahid Ahmed |work=[[HuffPost]] |date=10 August 2016 |access-date=8 October 2016 |archive-date=14 December 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181214075841/https://www.huffingtonpost.com/entry/barack-obama-could-end-the-killing-in-yemen-in-hours_us_57f7d5dee4b0b6a43031ba4e |url-status=live }}</ref> accelerated the sale of weapons to coalition states,<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.mcclatchydc.com/2015/04/07/262381/us-stepping-up-weapons-shipments.html|publisher=[[The McClatchy Company|McClatchy DC]] |first=James|last=Rosen |author-link=James Rosen (journalist) |title=U.S. stepping up weapons shipments to aid Saudi air campaign over Yemen|date=7 April 2015|access-date=7 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150714113828/http://www.mcclatchydc.com/news/nation-world/world/middle-east/article24782785.html|archive-date=2015-07-14}}</ref> and continued [[List of drone strikes in Yemen|strikes against AQAP.]] American and British servicemen have provided advice and training related to Saudi-led airstrikes in Yemen.<ref name="theguardian-com_2016-01-15_BZM">{{cite news |first=Emma |last=Graham-Harrison |title=British and US military 'in command room' for Saudi strikes on Yemen |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=15 January 2016 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2016/jan/15/british-us-military-in-command-room-saudi-strikes-yemen |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160116170759/http://www.theguardian.com/world/2016/jan/15/british-us-military-in-command-room-saudi-strikes-yemen |archive-date=16 January 2016 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="theguardian-com_2016-01-19_BHS">{{cite news |first=Vickie |last=Hawkins |title=Bombing hospitals and schools cannot become the new normal |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=19 January 2016 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2016/jan/19/yemen-msf-bombing-hospitals-schools-cannot-become-the-new-normal |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160120140224/http://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2016/jan/19/yemen-msf-bombing-hospitals-schools-cannot-become-the-new-normal |archive-date=20 January 2016 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="publications-parliament-uk_2016-01-12_HoC">{{cite web |title=House of Commons debate, Tuesday 12 January 2016 |publisher=publications.parliament.uk (Daily Hansard – Debate, Column 681) |date=12 January 2016 |url=https://publications.parliament.uk/pa/cm201516/cmhansrd/cm160112/debtext/160112-0001.htm |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160121202400/http://www.publications.parliament.uk/pa/cm201516/cmhansrd/cm160112/debtext/160112-0001.htm |archive-date=21 January 2016 |url-status=live }}</ref>

The intervention drew criticism from some governments, international organizations, and human rights groups, which stated that it contributed to civilian casualties, damage to infrastructure, and the humanitarian situation in Yemen.<ref name="un-org_2015-07-28_DCN">{{cite news|url=https://www.un.org/apps/news/story.asp?NewsID=51516|title=Durable ceasefire needed as 'humanitarian catastrophe' leaves millions suffering in Yemen – UN relief chief)|date=28 July 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150729021147/http://www.un.org/apps/news/story.asp?NewsID=51516|archive-date=29 July 2015|url-status=live|publisher=UN News Centre}}</ref><ref name="auto9">{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2015/jun/05/saudi-led-naval-blockade-worsens-yemen-humanitarian-disaster |newspaper=[[The Guardian]] |title=Saudi-led naval blockade leaves 20&nbsp;m Yemenis facing humanitarian disaster |date=5 June 2015 |first=Julian |last=Borger |author-link=Julian Borger |access-date=31 October 2015 |archive-date=16 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190516150910/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2015/jun/05/saudi-led-naval-blockade-worsens-yemen-humanitarian-disaster |url-status=live }}</ref> Academics also dispute whether it violates [[Use of force in international law|Article 2(4)]] of the [[Charter of the United Nations|UN Charter]].<ref>{{cite journal |last=Nußberger |first=Benjamin |title=Military strikes in Yemen in 2015: intervention by invitation and self-defence in the course of Yemen's 'model transitional process' |journal=Journal on the Use of Force and International Law |date=5 January 2017 |volume=4|pages=110–160 |doi=10.1080/20531702.2017.1256565 |s2cid=157698592 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.irinnews.org/analysis/2015/04/03/saudi-war-yemen-legal|title=Is the Saudi war on Yemen legal?|date=3 April 2015|publisher=IRIN|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=4 January 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190104151754/http://www.irinnews.org/analysis/2015/04/03/saudi-war-yemen-legal|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal|first1=Luca|last1=Ferro|first2=Tom|last2=Ruys|title=Weathering the Storm: Legality and Legal Implications of the Saudi-Led Military Intervention in Yemen|journal=International & Comparative Law Quarterly|volume=65|issue=1|pages=61–98|doi=10.1017/S0020589315000536|year=2016|s2cid=143061575 |url=https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/Delivery.cfm/SSRN_ID2685567_code1941279.pdf?abstractid=2685567&mirid=1 }}</ref> By 2019, the conflict was reported as a "military stalemate",<ref>{{cite news |title=Yemeni Separatists Relinquish Control of Buildings in Aden |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/17/world/middleeast/yemen-houthi-saudi-oilfield.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190817112003/https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/17/world/middleeast/yemen-houthi-saudi-oilfield.html |archive-date=2019-08-17 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |access-date=4 September 2019 |work=The New York Times |agency=Reuters |date=17 August 2019}}</ref> and the following year, Saudi Arabia declared its first unilateral ceasefire.<ref name="covid">{{cite news|url=https://www.theglobalist.com/yemen-war-saudi-arabia-iran-houthis-coronavirus-pandemic-covid19-war-crimes-islam/|title=The Saudis' War in Yemen Is a Crime Against Humanity|author=Ben-Meir, Alon|date=18 April 2020|work=[[The Globalist]]|access-date=25 April 2020|archive-date=22 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200422085110/https://www.theglobalist.com/yemen-war-saudi-arabia-iran-houthis-coronavirus-pandemic-covid19-war-crimes-islam/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Saudi Arabia and the War in Yemen - Riyadh's Retreat |url=https://magazine.zenith.me/en/politics/saudi-arabia-and-war-yemen |access-date=22 April 2020 |work=Zenith Magazine |date=21 April 2020 |archive-date=30 April 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200430004752/https://magazine.zenith.me/en/politics/saudi-arabia-and-war-yemen |url-status=live }}</ref> On 29 March 2022, the Saudi-led coalition announced that it would cease all hostilities within Yemen to facilitate political talks and peacekeeping efforts;<ref>{{cite news |last=Gambrell |first=Jon |title=Saudi-led coalition declares unilateral cease-fire in Yemen |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/gulf-states-plan-yemen-talks-without-houthi-rebels-present/2022/03/29/3bfa7fc0-af23-11ec-9dbd-0d4609d44c1c_story.html |quote=The coalition said it would cease hostilities in the brutal war starting at 6 a.m. Wednesday seeking to create a fertile environment for political talks and to jumpstart peacemaking efforts during the holy Muslim month of Ramadan. |newspaper=The Washington Post |access-date=29 March 2022 |date=29 March 2022 |archive-date=29 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220329055143/https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/gulf-states-plan-yemen-talks-without-houthi-rebels-present/2022/03/29/3bfa7fc0-af23-11ec-9dbd-0d4609d44c1c_story.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Houthi and Saudi officials subsequently began bilateral peace talks mediated by [[Oman]] under UN auspices, and most restrictions on commercial goods were lifted by April 2023.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=Actor Profile: Saudi-led Coalition {{!}} Yemen Conflict Observatory |url=https://acleddata.com/yemen-conflict-observatory/actor-profiles/saudi-led-coalition/ |access-date=2024-04-01 |website=ACLED |date=20 November 2023 |language=en-US |archive-date=13 September 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240913004056/https://acleddata.com/yemen-conflict-observatory/actor-profiles/saudi-led-coalition/ |url-status=live }}</ref> As of April 2024, open hostilities have largely ceased, though negotiations are ongoing due to complications caused by [[Red Sea crisis|Houthi attacks on Red Sea shipping]] since October 2023.<ref name=":0" />{{toclimit|3}}

== Background ==
{{See also|Saudi Arabia–Yemen relations|Houthi insurgency|Aftermath of the Houthi takeover in Yemen}}

Saudi-backed [[Abdrabbuh Mansur Hadi]], running unopposed as the only candidate for president, won the [[2012 Yemeni presidential election|2012 Yemeni elections]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-22096125|title=Yemen President Hadi 'removes Republican Guard commander'|publisher=BBC News|date=10 April 2013|access-date=12 April 2015|archive-date=16 January 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190116003231/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-22096125|url-status=live}}</ref> Since August 2014, the [[Houthis]], also known as Ansar Allah, a [[Zaidiyyah|Zaidi]] [[Shia Islam|Shia]] movement and militant group backed by [[Iran]], organized mass protests against the Hadi government's decisions and the new constitution. The protests culminated into the [[Houthi takeover in Yemen|Houthi takeover of the Yemeni government]] in 2015, after which the Houthis declared victory for the revolution and began drafting a new constitution, saying that Hadi's provisional government's term had already expired. Saudi Arabia and other countries denounced this as an unconstitutional coup d'état.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-yemen-security-gcc-idUKKBN0KU1YX20150121|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151208095438/http://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-yemen-security-gcc-idUKKBN0KU1YX20150121|archive-date=8 December 2015|title=Gulf Arab ministers condemn Houthi 'coup' in Yemen|publisher=[[Reuters]]|date=21 January 2015|access-date=5 May 2015}}</ref> The Houthis were supported by sections of the Yemeni armed forces loyal to the former president [[Ali Abdullah Saleh]], who was removed from power as part of the 2011 Arab Spring uprisings<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/rebels-push-offensive-in-aden-killing-dozens-with-artillery-fire/2015/03/31/79f53d9e-d729-11e4-bf0b-f648b95a6488_story.html|title=Yemen's Houthi Rebels get boost from country's ousted dictator|date=31 March 2015|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|first=Ali|last=al-Mujahed|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=17 May 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190517201729/https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/rebels-push-offensive-in-aden-killing-dozens-with-artillery-fire/2015/03/31/79f53d9e-d729-11e4-bf0b-f648b95a6488_story.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.middleeasteye.net/news/yemen-leak-collusion-between-houthis-and-ex-president-saleh-1125273454|title=Yemen leak: Collusion between Houthis and ex-president Saleh|publisher=[[Middle East Eye]]|date=22 January 2015|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=21 January 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190121064207/https://www.middleeasteye.net/news/yemen-leak-collusion-between-houthis-and-ex-president-saleh-1125273454|url-status=live}}</ref> but was later killed by his former Houthi allies.<ref>{{cite web | title=Ali Abdullah Saleh, Yemen's former leader, killed in Sanaa | website=BBC Home | date=2017-12-04 | url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-42225574 | access-date=2026-07-22}}</ref>

By September 2014, Houthi fighters captured [[Sanaa]], toppling Hadi's government. Soon after, a peace deal (known as the 'Peace and Partnership Agreement') was sealed between the Hadi government and the Houthis, but was not honored by either party. The deal was drafted with the intent of defining a power-sharing government. A conflict over a draft constitution resulted in the Houthis consolidating control over the Yemeni capital in January 2015. After resigning from his post alongside his prime minister and remaining under virtual house arrest for one month, Hadi fled to [[Aden]] in [[South Yemen|southern Yemen]] in February.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.crisisgroup.org/en/regions/middle-east-north-africa/iraq-iran-gulf/yemen/b045-yemen-at-war.aspx |title=Yemen at War |publisher=[[International Crisis Group]] |date=27 March 2015 |access-date=6 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150403051309/http://www.crisisgroup.org/en/regions/middle-east-north-africa/iraq-iran-gulf/yemen/b045-yemen-at-war.aspx|archive-date=2015-04-03}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Heinze|first=Marie-Christine|url=http://en.qantara.de/content/the-crisis-in-yemen-the-primacy-of-stability-over-real-change|title=The crisis in Yemen – The primacy of stability over real change|website=[[Qantara.de]]|date=30 January 2015|access-date=12 April 2015|archive-date=6 February 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150206053146/http://en.qantara.de/content/the-crisis-in-yemen-the-primacy-of-stability-over-real-change|url-status=live}}</ref> Upon arriving in Aden, Hadi withdrew his resignation, saying that the actions of the Houthis from September 2014 had amounted to a "coup" against him.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.middleeasteye.net/news/yemens-hadi-withdraws-resignation-un-pushes-dialogue-1907885482|title=Yemen's Hadi withdraws resignation, as UN pushes for dialogue|date=24 February 2015|publisher=[[Middle East Eye]]|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=4 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150404002035/http://www.middleeasteye.net/news/yemens-hadi-withdraws-resignation-un-pushes-dialogue-1907885482|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.aljazeera.com/news/2015/02/yemen-abd-rabbu-masnour-hadi-150224001628332.html|title=Yemen's ousted president withdraws resignation|date=24 February 2015|publisher=[[Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]]|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=14 May 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150514082932/http://www.aljazeera.com/news/2015/02/yemen-abd-rabbu-masnour-hadi-150224001628332.html|url-status=live}}</ref> By 25 March, forces answering to Sanaa were [[Battle of Aden (2015)|rapidly closing in on Aden]], which Hadi had declared to be Yemen's temporary capital.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.yementimes.com/en/1871/news/4999/Al-Subaihi-captured-and-Lahj-falls-as-Houthis-move-on-Aden.htm|newspaper=[[Yemen Times]] |title=Al Subaihi captured and Lahj falls as Houthis move on Aden|date=25 March 2015|access-date=26 March 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150326092326/http://www.yementimes.com/en/1871/news/4999/Al-Subaihi-captured-and-Lahj-falls-as-Houthis-move-on-Aden.htm|archive-date=2015-03-26}}</ref>

During the [[Yemeni Civil War (2015–present)#Southern offensive|Houthis' southern offensive]], Saudi Arabia began a military buildup on its [[Saudi Arabia–Yemen border|border]] with Yemen.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-usa-saudi-idUSKBN0MK2S120150324|publisher=[[Reuters]]|title=Exclusive: Saudi Arabia building up military near Yemen border – U.S. officials|date=24 March 2015|access-date=25 March 2015}}</ref> In response, a Houthi commander boasted that his troops would counterattack against any Saudi aggression and would not stop until they had taken [[Riyadh]], the Saudi capital.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/yemen-protests-against-militants-leave-one-dead-17-injured-1427193838|work=[[The Wall Street Journal]]|title=Yemen's Houthi Militants Extend Push Southward|last=Almasmari|first=Hakim|author-link=Hakim Almasmari|date=24 March 2015|access-date=25 March 2015|archive-date=24 March 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150324222255/http://www.wsj.com/articles/yemen-protests-against-militants-leave-one-dead-17-injured-1427193838|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 25 March, Hadi called on the UN Security Council to authorise "willing countries that wish to help Yemen to provide immediate support for the legitimate authority by all means and measures to protect Yemen and deter the Houthi aggression".<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-32045984 |title=Yemen's President Hadi asks UN to back intervention |date=25 March 2015 |publisher=BBC News |access-date=13 September 2024 |archive-date=20 January 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240120084619/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-32045984 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Yemen's foreign minister, [[Riad Yassin]], requested military assistance from the [[Arab League]] on 25 March, amid reports that Hadi had fled his provisional capital.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.vice.com/article/with-a-bounty-on-his-head-yemens-president-flees-as-rebels-advance|website=[[Vice News]]|title=Saudi Arabia Launches Airstrikes in Yemen as President Flees Amid Rebel Advance|date=25 March 2015|first=John|last=Beck|access-date=25 March 2015|archive-date=11 July 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150711142547/https://news.vice.com/article/with-a-bounty-on-his-head-yemens-president-flees-as-rebels-advance}}</ref><ref>{{in lang|vi}} [http://www.nguoi-viet.com/absolutenm2/templates/viewarticlesNVO.aspx?articleid=204942&zoneid=5 "Phiến quân Shiite tấn công, tổng thống Yemen bỏ chạy"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150402123101/http://www.nguoi-viet.com/absolutenm2/templates/viewarticlesNVO.aspx?articleid=204942&zoneid=5 |date=2 April 2015 }}. 25 March 2015. Retrieved 26 March 2015</ref> On 26 March, Saudi state TV station [[Al-Ekhbariya TV]] reported that Hadi arrived at a Riyadh airbase and was met by Saudi defense minister [[Mohammed bin Salman]]. His route from Aden to Riyadh was not immediately known.<ref name="CBC News">{{cite news|date=26 March 2015|title=Yemeni's Abed Rabbo Mansour Hadi arrives in Saudi capital|publisher=[[CBC News]]|url=http://www.cbc.ca/news/world/yemeni-s-abed-rabbo-mansour-hadi-arrives-in-saudi-capital-1.3010698|access-date=26 March 2015|archive-date=21 July 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210721024845/https://www.cbc.ca/news/world/yemeni-s-abed-rabbo-mansour-hadi-arrives-in-saudi-capital-1.3010698|url-status=live}}</ref>

At a summit of the Arab League held in Sharm El-Sheikh, Egypt, on 28–29 March, President Hadi again repeated his calls for international intervention in the fighting. A number of League members pledged their support to Hadi's government during that meeting.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.dw.de/arab-league-summit-wraps-up-with-plan-for-pan-arab-force/a-18348096|date=29 March 2015|title=Arab League summit wraps up with plan for pan-Arab force|publisher=[[Deutsche Welle]]|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=17 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150417142332/http://www.dw.de/arab-league-summit-wraps-up-with-plan-for-pan-arab-force/a-18348096|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.dw.de/yemen-crisis-tops-agenda-as-arab-league-summit-opens/a-18346872|date=28 March 2015|title=Yemen crisis tops agenda as Arab League summit opens|publisher=[[Deutsche Welle]]|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=17 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150417141853/http://www.dw.de/yemen-crisis-tops-agenda-as-arab-league-summit-opens/a-18346872|url-status=live}}</ref>

After more than seven years of hostilities that left thousands of civilians dead or injured and devastated [[Yemen]], a surprise deal was agreed between regional rivals Saudi Arabia and Iran in March 2023. The deal aimed to restore diplomatic relations that could end the Saudi war in Yemen. Experts raised a warning that the war could worsen instead of ending, if Saudi Arabia agrees to end military operations.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://edition.cnn.com/2023/03/22/middleeast/yemen-war-saudi-iran-mime-intl/index.html|title=A Saudi-Iran reconciliation may not end the war in Yemen just yet|access-date=22 March 2023|work=CNN|date=22 March 2023|archive-date=22 March 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230322232531/https://edition.cnn.com/2023/03/22/middleeast/yemen-war-saudi-iran-mime-intl/index.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

== Operation Decisive Storm ==
{{distinguish|Operation Desert Storm}}
According to the Saudi news outlet [[Al Arabiya]], Saudi Arabia contributed 100 warplanes and 150,000 soldiers to the military operation. Several media agencies reported that planes from [[Egypt]], [[Morocco]], [[Jordan]], [[Sudan]], [[Kuwait]], the [[United Arab Emirates]], [[Qatar]] and [[Bahrain]] were taking part.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-coalition-factbox-idUSKBN0N11F220150410|title=Factbox: Saudi-led coalition against Yemen's Houthis|date=10 April 2015|publisher=[[Reuters]]|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=18 April 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230418144033/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-coalition-factbox-idUSKBN0N11F220150410|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2015/03/25/middleeast/yemen-unrest/ |title=Saudi Arabia launches airstrikes in Yemen |author=Steve Almasy |author2=Jason Hanna |work=CNN|date=25 March 2015 |access-date=26 March 2015 |archive-date=26 March 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150326010115/http://edition.cnn.com/2015/03/25/middleeast/yemen-unrest/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="strength">{{cite news|url=http://www.aljazeera.com/news/middleeast/2015/03/saudi-ambassador-announces-military-operation-yemen-150325234138956.html|title=Saudi and Arab allies bomb Houthi positions in Yemen|date=26 March 2015|access-date=26 March 2014|archive-date=26 March 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150326181207/http://www.aljazeera.com/news/middleeast/2015/03/saudi-ambassador-announces-military-operation-yemen-150325234138956.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="repel2">{{cite news|url=http://english.alarabiya.net/en/News/middle-east/2015/03/26/GCC-states-to-repel-Houthi-aggression-in-Yemen-statement-.html|title=Saudi warplanes bomb Houthi positions in Yemen|date=25 March 2015|access-date=25 March 2015|website=[[Al Arabiya English]]|archive-date=27 March 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150327054325/http://english.alarabiya.net/en/News/middle-east/2015/03/26/GCC-states-to-repel-Houthi-aggression-in-Yemen-statement-.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="sudan">{{cite news|url=http://www.worldbulletin.net/todays-news/157087/sudan-denies-plane-shot-down-by-yemens-houthis|website=World Bulletin|title=Sudan denies plane shot down by Yemen's Houthis|date=28 March 2015|access-date=28 March 2015|archive-date=30 March 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150330062026/http://www.worldbulletin.net/todays-news/157087/sudan-denies-plane-shot-down-by-yemens-houthis|url-status=usurped}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2015/mar/26/gulf-states-yemen-ground-offensive-to-halt-houthi-rebel-advance|title=Gulf states consider Yemen ground offensive to halt Houthi rebel advance|date=26 March 2015|work=[[The Guardian]]|access-date=26 March 2014|archive-date=27 March 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150327044637/http://www.theguardian.com/world/2015/mar/26/gulf-states-yemen-ground-offensive-to-halt-houthi-rebel-advance|url-status=live}}</ref>{{Excessive citations inline|reason=The large number of citations here clutters the article up.|date=April 2023}} Egypt had previously sent four warships supporting the Saudi naval blockade.<ref name="sites.tufts.edu">{{Cite web|url=https://sites.tufts.edu/reinventingpeace/2018/02/28/who-is-arming-the-yemen-war-and-is-anyone-planning-to-stop/|title=Who is arming the Yemen war? (And is anyone planning to stop?) |publisher=Reinventing Peace |date=28 February 2018 |access-date=2019-02-16 |archive-date=24 February 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190224192131/https://sites.tufts.edu/reinventingpeace/2018/02/28/who-is-arming-the-yemen-war-and-is-anyone-planning-to-stop/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

The operation was declared over on 21 April 2015.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-32402688?post_id=100001523553823_889997584394352#_=_|title=Yemen conflict: Saudi Arabia ends air campaign|publisher=BBC News|date=21 April 2015|access-date=21 April 2015|archive-date=10 August 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150810195802/http://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-32402688?post_id=100001523553823_889997584394352#_=_|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Air campaign ===

==== March 2015 ====
In March 2015, in a joint statement, the member states of the [[Gulf Cooperation Council]] (with the exception of [[Oman]]) said they had decided to intervene against the Houthis at the request of Hadi's government.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.aljazeera.com/news/middleeast/2015/03/saudi-ambassador-announces-military-operation-yemen-150325234138956.html|publisher=[[Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]]|title=Saudi and Arab allies bomb Houthi positions in Yemen|date=26 March 2015|access-date=25 March 2015|archive-date=26 March 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150326181207/http://www.aljazeera.com/news/middleeast/2015/03/saudi-ambassador-announces-military-operation-yemen-150325234138956.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

The coalition declared Yemeni airspace to be a restricted area, with [[Salman of Saudi Arabia|King Salman]] declaring the [[Royal Saudi Air Force]] to be [[No-fly zone|in full control of the zone]].<ref name="repel2"/> Saudi Arabia began airstrikes, reportedly relying on US intelligence reports and surveillance images to select and hit targets, including weapons, aircraft<ref name="cnn">{{cite news|url=http://www.cnn.com/2015/03/25/middleeast/yemen-unrest/|work=CNN|title=Saudi Arabia launches airstrikes in Yemen|date=26 March 2015|access-date=25 March 2015|archive-date=20 June 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190620050554/https://www.cnn.com/2015/03/25/middleeast/yemen-unrest/|url-status=live}}</ref> on the ground and air defences.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/national-security/officials-saudi-led-action-in-yemen-relied-heavily-on-us-intelligence/2015/03/26/6d15302c-d3da-11e4-8fce-3941fc548f1c_story.html|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|title=Officials: Saudi-led action relied on U.S. intelligence|last=DeYoung|first=Karen|author-link=Karen DeYoung|date=26 March 2015|access-date=7 April 2015|archive-date=3 May 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180503040658/https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/national-security/officials-saudi-led-action-in-yemen-relied-heavily-on-us-intelligence/2015/03/26/6d15302c-d3da-11e4-8fce-3941fc548f1c_story.html|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Al Jazeera Media Network|Al Jazeera]] reported that [[Mohammed Ali al-Houthi]], a Houthi commander appointed in February as president of the [[Revolutionary Committee (Yemen)|Revolutionary Committee]], was injured and three other Houthi commanders were killed by airstrikes in Sanaa.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://america.aljazeera.com/articles/2015/3/25/houthi-aden.html|publisher=[[Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]]|title=Saudis launch air campaign to defend Yemen government|date=25 March 2015|access-date=1 April 2015|archive-date=2 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150402141530/http://america.aljazeera.com/articles/2015/3/25/houthi-aden.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

Strikes on 26 March also hit [[Al Anad Air Base]], a former [[United States special operations forces|US special operations forces]] facility in [[Lahij Governorate]] seized by Houthis earlier in the week.<ref name="leads">{{cite news|url=http://america.aljazeera.com/articles/2015/3/26/Saudi-airstrikes-yemen.html|title=Saudi Arabia leads airstrikes against Yemen's Houthi rebels|date=26 March 2015|publisher=[[Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]] America|access-date=26 March 2015|archive-date=29 March 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150329011438/http://america.aljazeera.com/articles/2015/3/26/Saudi-airstrikes-yemen.html|url-status=live}}</ref> The targets reportedly included the Houthi-controlled missile base in Sanaa and its fuel depot.<ref name="offensive2">{{cite news |url=https://www.theglobeandmail.com/news/world/saudi-arabia-has-150000-troops-for-yemen-operation-report/article23628188/ |newspaper=[[The Globe and Mail]] |title=Egypt, Jordan and Sudan ready for ground offensive in Yemen.: report |date=26 March 2015 |location=Toronto |access-date=26 March 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150326221558/http://www.theglobeandmail.com/news/world/saudi-arabia-has-150000-troops-for-yemen-operation-report/article23628188/ |archive-date=26 March 2015 }}</ref> Strikes overnight also targeted Houthis in [[Taiz]] and [[Saada]]. Thousands demonstrated in Sanaa against the intervention, which ex-president [[Ali Abdullah Saleh]] also condemned. In Taiz thousands came out supporting Hadi and Saudi Arabia.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.aljazeera.com/news/2015/03/coalition-jets-continue-hit-houthi-targets-yemen-150327022702372.html|publisher=[[Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]]|title=Coalition jets continue to hit Houthi targets in Yemen|date=27 March 2015|access-date=26 March 2015|archive-date=2 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150402134533/http://www.aljazeera.com/news/2015/03/coalition-jets-continue-hit-houthi-targets-yemen-150327022702372.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

The scope of strikes expanded further on 27 March, with a radar installation in the [[Marib Governorate]] and an airbase in the [[Abyan Governorate]] coming under air attack. The commander of the operation dismissed reports of civilian casualties, saying airstrikes were being carried out with precision.<ref name="voa">{{cite news|url=https://www.voanews.com/a/saudi-led-airstrikes-continue-for-second-day-in-yemen/2696496.html|publisher=[[Voice of America]]|title=Egyptian, Saudi Vessels Approach Yemen Coast|first=Edward|last=Yeranian|date=27 March 2015|access-date=27 March 2015|archive-date=28 March 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150328010125/http://www.voanews.com/content/saudi-led-airstrikes-continue-for-second-day-in-yemen/2696496.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Additional strikes early on the next day hit targets in [[Hodeidah]], Saada and the Sanaa area, as well as [[Ali Abdullah Saleh]]'s main base. Rumours indicated Saleh fled to [[Sanhan, Yemen|Sanhan]], on the outskirts of the Houthi-controlled capital.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.aljazeera.com/news/2015/03/yemen-aden-saudi-arabia-150327174924579.html|publisher=[[Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]]|title=Saudi-led air strikes hit Yemen for third night|date=28 March 2015|access-date=27 March 2015|archive-date=6 December 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171206064838/http://www.aljazeera.com/news/2015/03/yemen-aden-saudi-arabia-150327174924579.html|url-status=live}}</ref> An Aden government official said Saudi strikes destroyed a long-range missile facility controlled by the Houthis.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.worldbulletin.net/todays-news/157098/saudi-operation-hit-yemen-base-holding-long-range-missiles |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150330062047/http://www.worldbulletin.net/todays-news/157098/saudi-operation-hit-yemen-base-holding-long-range-missiles |url-status=usurped |archive-date=30 March 2015 |date=28 March 2015|website=World Bulletin|title=Saudi operation hit Yemen base holding long-range missiles|access-date=28 March 2015}}</ref>

The Houthis claimed to have shot down a [[Sudanese Air Force]] plane over northern Sanaa and captured its pilot on 28 March. The Sudanese government denied that any of its four warplanes had come under fire or been shot down.<ref name="sudan"/> On the previous day, the Houthis claimed to have shot down a "hostile" Saudi drone in Sanaa.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.aa.com.tr/en/world/yemens-houthis-shoot-down-drone-in-sanaa-tv/63148|title=Yemen's Houthis shoot down drone in Sanaa: TV|publisher=Anadolu Agency |access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=13 September 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240913010800/https://www.aa.com.tr/en/world/yemens-houthis-shoot-down-drone-in-sanaa-tv/63148|url-status=live}}</ref>

Airstrikes hit an arms depot, military airbase and special forces headquarters in Sanaa early on 29 March. A weapons depot outside Sanaa was destroyed, causing damage to an airport and planes on the ground. Saada and Hodeidah were targeted as well. Brigadier General [[Ahmad Asiri (military)|Ahmed Asiri]], the coalition's spokesman, said Saudi artillery and [[Boeing AH-64 Apache|Apache]] attack helicopters were mobilised to "deter" Houthi fighters massing on the border with Saudi Arabia.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.aljazeera.com/news/2015/03/fresh-saudi-led-strikes-hit-houthi-targets-yemen-150328224634577.html|publisher=[[Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]]|date=29 March 2015|title=Fresh Saudi-led strikes hit Houthi targets in Yemen|access-date=28 March 2015|archive-date=25 May 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190525045549/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2015/03/fresh-saudi-led-strikes-hit-houthi-targets-yemen-150328224634577.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 30 March, at least 40 people including children were killed and 200 were injured<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/middle-east/saudi-arabia-accused-of-killing-40-including-children-in-air-strike-on-yemen-refugee-camp-10145294.html|title=Saudi Arabia accused of killing 40 including children in air strike on Yemen refugee camp|work=[[The Independent]]|location=London|first=Lizzie|last=Dearden|date=31 March 2015|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=27 December 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171227142826/http://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/middle-east/saudi-arabia-accused-of-killing-40-including-children-in-air-strike-on-yemen-refugee-camp-10145294.html|url-status=live}}</ref> by an airstrike that hit the Al-Mazraq refugee camp near a military installation in northern district of [[Haradh]], international organizations said. Airstrikes also hit areas near the presidential palace in Sanaa,<ref>{{cite news|url=https://abcnews.go.com/International/wireStory/airstrikes-shake-yemens-capital-shiite-rebels-push-south-30000813|publisher=[[ABC News (United States)|ABC News]]|title=Yemeni Rebels Shell Aden as Saudi Launches More Airstrikes|first=Ahmed|last=Al-Haj|date=30 March 2015|access-date=30 March 2015|archive-date=12 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150412125519/http://abcnews.go.com/International/wireStory/airstrikes-shake-yemens-capital-shiite-rebels-push-south-30000813|url-status=live}}</ref> as well as [[Aden International Airport]].<ref name="dozens">{{cite news|url=https://www.smh.com.au/world/dozens-killed-as-yemens-houthis-shell-aden-saudi-jets-bomb-airport-20150331-1mccn3.html|newspaper=[[The Sydney Morning Herald]]|title=Dozens killed as Yemen's Houthis shell Aden, Saudi jets bomb airport|date=31 March 2015|access-date=31 March 2015|archive-date=13 October 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171013183834/http://www.smh.com.au/world/dozens-killed-as-yemens-houthis-shell-aden-saudi-jets-bomb-airport-20150331-1mccn3.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

Food storage of Yemen Economic Corporation in Hodeidah was destroyed by three coalition strikes on 31 March.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.thenational.ae/world/middle-east/yemeni-civilians-struggle-to-get-by-amid-conflict|title=Yemeni civilians struggle to get by amid conflict|date=6 April 2015|access-date=8 April 2015|archive-date=25 January 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170125190627/http://www.thenational.ae/world/middle-east/yemeni-civilians-struggle-to-get-by-amid-conflict|url-status=live}}</ref> Airstrikes were not limited to the Yemeni mainland. Missiles struck homes on the island of [[Perim]], according to residents who fled by boat to [[Djibouti]].<ref>{{cite news|work=CNN|url=http://www.cnn.com/2015/04/09/middleeast/yemen-refugees-djibouti/index.html|date=9 April 2015|title='A window into hell:' Desperate Yemenis flee Saudi airstrikes by boat|first=Nima|last=Elbagir|author-link=Nima Elbagir|access-date=9 April 2015|archive-date=9 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150409165619/http://www.cnn.com/2015/04/09/middleeast/yemen-refugees-djibouti/index.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

==== April 2015 ====
