Dozens of casualties came from an explosion in a dairy and oil factory in [[Hodeidah]], which was variously blamed on an airstrike or a rocket from a nearby military base on 1 April. Medical sources reported 25 deaths, while the Yemen Army said 37 were killed and 80 wounded.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-factory-idUSKBN0MS3H020150401|publisher=[[Reuters]]|title=Explosion at Yemen factory kills at least 25: residents, medics|date=1 April 2015|access-date=1 April 2015|archive-date=27 January 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160127230727/http://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-factory-idUSKBN0MS3H020150401|url-status=live}}</ref> Airstrikes also hit targets in Saada on 1 April.<ref name="rages">{{cite news|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2015-03-31/yemen-rebels-seize-base-near-shipping-lane-as-saudis-target-aden|publisher=[[Bloomberg News]]|title=Saudi Coalition Hits Houthi Stronghold as Aden Battle Rages|date=31 March 2015|first=Mohammed|last=Hatem|access-date=1 April 2015|archive-date=2 July 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190702134341/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2015-03-31/yemen-rebels-seize-base-near-shipping-lane-as-saudis-target-aden|url-status=live}}</ref>

Despite persistent airstrikes, Houthi and allied units continued to advance on central Aden, backed by tanks and heavy artillery.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/death-toll-in-yemen-rises-as-saudi-led-military-attacks-continue/2015/04/01/12feec94-d7f0-11e4-b3f2-607bd612aeac_story.html|title=Rebel forces push farther into key Yemeni port city of Aden|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|first=Ali|last=al-Mujahed|date=1 April 2015|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=8 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210908100750/https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/death-toll-in-yemen-rises-as-saudi-led-military-attacks-continue/2015/04/01/12feec94-d7f0-11e4-b3f2-607bd612aeac_story.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-idUSKBN0MS4Y120150401|title=Yemen Houthi fighters backed by tanks reach central Aden|publisher=[[Reuters]]|date=1 April 2015|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=6 October 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151006080305/http://www.reuters.com/article/2015/04/01/us-yemen-security-idUSKBN0MS4Y120150401|url-status=live}}</ref> Houthis seized the presidential palace on 2 April, but reportedly withdrew after overnight air raids early the next day.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://zeenews.india.com/news/world/yemen-rebels-quit-aden-palace-after-air-raids-senior-official_1572403.html|publisher=[[Zee News]]|title=Yemen rebels quit Aden palace after air raids: Senior official|date=3 April 2015|access-date=2 April 2015|archive-date=23 December 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181223163910/http://zeenews.india.com/news/world/yemen-rebels-quit-aden-palace-after-air-raids-senior-official_1572403.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Coalition planes also airdropped weapons and medical aid to pro-Hadi fighters in Aden.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/saudis-airdrop-arms-to-aden-defenders-houthis-pull-back/article1-1333664.aspx|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150405005828/http://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/saudis-airdrop-arms-to-aden-defenders-houthis-pull-back/article1-1333664.aspx|archive-date=5 April 2015|newspaper=[[Hindustan Times]] |title=Saudis airdrop arms to Aden defenders, Houthis pull back|date=3 April 2015|access-date=3 April 2015}}</ref>

The [[International Committee of the Red Cross]] (ICRC) announced on 5 April that it had received permission from the coalition to fly medical supplies and aid workers into Sanaa and was awaiting permission to send a surgical team by boat to [[Aden]]. The coalition said it had set up a special body to coordinate aid deliveries to Yemen.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.aljazeera.com/news/2015/04/red-cross-permission-deliver-aid-yemen-150406011849828.html|publisher=[[Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]]|title=Red Cross given permission to deliver aid to Yemen|date=6 April 2015|access-date=5 April 2015|archive-date=7 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150407034656/http://www.aljazeera.com/news/2015/04/red-cross-permission-deliver-aid-yemen-150406011849828.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 6 April, airstrikes began before sunset and struck targets in western Sanaa, Saada and the [[Ad Dali' Governorate]], a supply route for Houthis in the [[Battle of Aden (2015)|Battle of Aden]].<ref name="fiercefighting">{{cite news|url=http://www.chron.com/news/world/article/Pakistan-says-Saudi-led-coalition-in-Yemen-wants-6180919.php|newspaper=[[Houston Chronicle]]|title=Fierce fighting as rebels move on holdouts in Yemen's Aden|date=6 April 2015|access-date=6 April 2015|archive-date=6 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150406215725/http://www.chron.com/news/world/article/Pakistan-says-Saudi-led-coalition-in-Yemen-wants-6180919.php}}</ref>

Airstrikes on 7 April hit a [[Republican Guard (Yemen)|Republican Guard]] base in the [[Ibb Governorate]], injuring 25 troops. Yemeni sources claimed three children at a nearby school were killed by the attack,<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.bostonherald.com/news_opinion/international/middle_east/2015/04/saudi_led_airstrikes_hit_yemens_south_amid_ground|newspaper=[[Boston Herald]]|title=Saudi-led airstrikes hit Yemen's south amid ground fighting|date=7 April 2015|access-date=7 April 2015|archive-date=28 March 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200328041736/http://www.bostonherald.com/news_opinion/international/middle_east/2015/04/saudi_led_airstrikes_hit_yemens_south_amid_ground|url-status=live}}</ref> while six were injured.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.cnn.com/2015/04/07/middleeast/yemen-crisis-houthis-saudi-arabia/|work=CNN|title=Yemen says Saudi airstrikes hit school, injuring students|date=7 April 2015|access-date=7 April 2015|archive-date=10 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150410073102/http://www.cnn.com/2015/04/07/middleeast/yemen-crisis-houthis-saudi-arabia|url-status=live}}</ref>

The [[Parliament of Pakistan]] voted against military action on 10 April, despite a request from Saudi Arabia that it join the coalition.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-idUSKBN0N10LO20150410|publisher=[[Reuters]]|title=Pakistan declines Saudi call for armed support in Yemen fight|date=10 April 2015|first=Mohammed|last=Mukhashaf|access-date=10 April 2015|archive-date=11 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150411000913/http://www.reuters.com/article/2015/04/10/us-yemen-security-idUSKBN0N10LO20150410|url-status=live}}</ref>

Airstrikes launched on 12 April, against the base of the 22nd Brigade of the [[Republican Guard (Yemen)|Yemeni Republican Guard]] in the [[Taiz Governorate]] struck both the brigade and a nearby village inhabited by members of the [[Al-Akhdam]] minority community, killing eight civilians and injuring more than ten others.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.yementimes.com/en/1875/news/5048/Civilians-killed-by-a-coalition-strike-on-a-Republican-Guards-Brigade-in-Taiz.htm|newspaper=[[Yemen Times]]|title=Civilians Killed by a Coalition Strike on a Republican Guards Brigade in Taiz|date=12 April 2015|access-date=12 April 2015|archive-date=25 July 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180725112125/http://www.yementimes.com/en/1875/news/5048/Civilians-killed-by-a-coalition-strike-on-a-Republican-Guards-Brigade-in-Taiz.htm}}</ref> On 17 April, both the GCC coalition's spokesman called by Saudi broadcaster Al-Ehkbariya TV and a commander of the pro-Hadi rebels on the ground said airstrikes had intensified, focusing on both Sanaa and Taiz.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://abcnews.go.com/International/wireStory/calls-274-million-yemen-aid-humanitarian-30387289|title=Al-Qaida in Yemen Takes Massive Weapons Depot From Army|date=17 April 2015|publisher=[[ABC News (United States)|ABC News]]|agency=Associated Press|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=28 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150428094745/http://abcnews.go.com/International/wireStory/calls-274-million-yemen-aid-humanitarian-30387289|url-status=live}}</ref> One strike on the Republican Palace in [[Taiz]] killed 19 pro-Houthi gunmen.<ref>{{cite news|author1=Ahmed Al-Haj|title=Yemen militia aligned with President Hadi attacks Houthi-held base|url=https://www.thestar.com/news/world/2015/04/18/yemen-militia-aligned-with-president-hadi-attacks-houthi-held-base.html|newspaper=[[Toronto Star]]|date=18 April 2015|access-date=19 April 2015|archive-date=19 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150419234708/http://www.thestar.com/news/world/2015/04/18/yemen-militia-aligned-with-president-hadi-attacks-houthi-held-base.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[File:Yemen ethno 2002.jpg|thumb|upright=1.45|Ethnoreligious groups in 2002. [[Zaidiyyah|Zaydi]] [[Shia]] followers make up between 35% and 42.1% of Muslims in Yemen.<ref>{{cite book|last=Day|first=Stephen W.|title=Regionalism and Rebellion in Yemen: A Troubled National Union|date=2012|page=31|publisher=Cambridge University Press|isbn=978-1-107-02215-7}}</ref>]]

=== Naval role ===
[[Egypt]] and Saudi Arabia committed warships to support coalition operations.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.militarytimes.com/story/military/2015/03/27/saudi-arabia-egypt-yemen-airstrikes/70545168/|work=[[Military Times Media Group|Military Times]]|first=Ahmed|last=al-Haj|title=Saudi, Egyptian warships move into strait as Yemen airstrikes widen|date=27 March 2015|access-date=7 April 2015|archive-date=22 January 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160122214747/http://www.militarytimes.com/story/military/2015/03/27/saudi-arabia-egypt-yemen-airstrikes/70545168/|url-status=live}}</ref> Somalia offered its airspace and territorial waters.<ref name="Ssfpstsciy"/> Four [[Egyptian Navy]] vessels steamed toward the [[Gulf of Aden]] after operations began.<ref name="leads"/> Riyadh requested access to Somali airspace and waters to carry out operations.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.somalicurrent.com/2015/03/26/saudi-led-coalition-request-somalia-to-use-its-airspace-to-attack-houthi-rebels/|work=Somali Current|title=Saudi led coalition request Somalia to use its airspace to attack Houthi rebels|date=26 March 2015|access-date=6 October 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150709131008/http://www.somalicurrent.com/2015/03/26/saudi-led-coalition-request-somalia-to-use-its-airspace-to-attack-houthi-rebels/ |archive-date=9 July 2015}}</ref> On 27 March, the [[military of Egypt|Egyptian military]] said a squadron of Egyptian and Saudi warships took up positions at the [[Bab al-Mandab]] strait.<ref name="voa"/> The Saudi military threatened to destroy any ship attempting to make port.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.cnn.com/2015/03/26/middleeast/yemen-saudi-arabia-airstrikes/|work=CNN|title=Saudi-led coalition strikes rebels in Yemen, inflaming tensions in region|date=26 March 2015|access-date=26 March 2015|archive-date=2 March 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190302002800/https://www.cnn.com/2015/03/26/middleeast/yemen-saudi-arabia-airstrikes/|url-status=live}}</ref>

The [[Royal Saudi Navy]] evacuated diplomats and United Nations staff from Aden to [[Jeddah]] on 28 March.<ref name="flee">{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-idUSKBN0ML0YC20150328|publisher=[[Reuters]]|title=Diplomats and U.N. staff flee Yemen as Houthis target Aden|date=28 March 2015|access-date=28 March 2015|archive-date=28 March 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150328060358/http://www.reuters.com/article/2015/03/28/us-yemen-security-idUSKBN0ML0YC20150328|url-status=live}}</ref>

Witnesses told [[Reuters]] that Egyptian warships bombarded Houthi positions as they attempted to advance on Aden on 30 March.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.trust.org/item/20150330141004-371fq/?source=search|publisher=Thomson Reuters Foundation|title=Warships shell Houthis outside Yemeni city of Aden -witnesses|date=30 March 2015|access-date=30 March 2015|archive-date=14 July 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150714182348/http://www.trust.org/item/20150330141004-371fq/?source=search}}</ref> Warships again fired on Houthi positions at Aden International Airport on or about 1 April.<ref name="rages"/>

Djibouti foreign minister [[Mahmoud Ali Youssouf]] said the Houthis placed heavy weapons and fast attack boats on [[Perim]] and a smaller island in the Bab al-Mandab strait. He warned "the prospect of a war in the strait of Bab al-Mandab is a real one" and said the weapons posed "a big danger" to his country, commercial shipping traffic, and military vessels. He called on the coalition to clear the islands, which he said included missiles and long-range cannons.<ref name="youssouf">{{cite news |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2015-04-02/yemeni-rebels-strengthen-positions-in-key-strait-djibouti-says |publisher=[[Bloomberg News]] |first=Paul |last=Richardson |date=2 April 2015 |title=Yemeni Rebels Strengthen Positions at Entrance to Red Sea |access-date=2 April 2015 |archive-date=3 April 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150403070312/http://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2015-04-02/yemeni-rebels-strengthen-positions-in-key-strait-djibouti-says |url-status=live }}</ref>

On 4 April, Egyptian President [[Abdel Fattah el-Sisi]] called protecting [[Red Sea]] shipping and securing the Bab al-Mandab "a top priority for Egypt's national security".<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.timesofoman.com/News/49836/Article-Securing-key-Yemen-strait-a-priority-for-Egypt-says-Sisi|newspaper=[[Times of Oman]]|title=Securing key Yemen strait a priority for Egypt, says Sisi|date=4 April 2015|access-date=7 April 2015|archive-date=16 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150416172514/http://www.timesofoman.com/News/49836/Article-Securing-key-Yemen-strait-a-priority-for-Egypt-says-Sisi}}</ref>

On 15 April, coalition spokesman Saudi Brigadier General Ahmed Al-Asiri, said that its warships were focusing on protecting shipping routes and screening ships heading to port for shipments intended for the Houthis.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.operationdecisivestorm.com/day-22-april-15-2015-saudi-ministry-of-defense-briefing/#sthash.oPYRw15c.dpbs|title=Day 21 April 15, 2015: Saudi Ministry of Defense Briefing|date=15 April 2015|publisher=Saudi Ministry of Defense}}</ref>

The [[United States Navy|US Navy]] provided support to the naval blockade, halting and searching vessels suspected of carrying Iranian arms to the Houthis.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/u-s-widens-role-in-saudi-led-campaign-against-yemen-rebels-1428882967|newspaper=[[The Wall Street Journal]]|title=U.S. Widens Role in Saudi-led Campaign Against Houthi Rebels in Yemen|date=12 April 2015|first1=Maria|last1=Abi-Habib|first2=Adam|last2=Entous|access-date=13 April 2015|archive-date=13 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150413054438/http://www.wsj.com/articles/u-s-widens-role-in-saudi-led-campaign-against-yemen-rebels-1428882967|url-status=live}}</ref> On 21 April, the United States announced it was deploying warships to Yemeni waters to monitor Iranian ships.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://edition.cnn.com/2015/04/20/politics/iran-united-states-warships-monitoring/|title=U.S.: Warships near Yemen create 'options' for dealing with Iranian vessels|work=CNN|date=21 April 2015|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=23 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150423024108/http://edition.cnn.com/2015/04/20/politics/iran-united-states-warships-monitoring|url-status=live}}</ref> The US in particular noted a convoy of Iranian vessels, which US authorities said could potentially be carrying weapons to Houthi fighters in contravention of UN sanctions.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://2009-2017.state.gov/r/pa/prs/dpb/2015/04/240950.htm#YEMEN|title=Daily Press Briefing for 21 April 2015|publisher=U.S. Department of State|date=21 April 2015|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=28 September 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230928085800/https://2009-2017.state.gov/r/pa/prs/dpb/2015/04/240950.htm#YEMEN|url-status=live}}</ref> The US reported that the Iranian convoy reversed course on 23 April.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/iranian-ships-turn-back-after-a-warning-about-arming-rebels-in-yemen-1429818885|title=Iranian Ships Turn Back After a Warning About Arming Rebels in Yemen|work=[[The Wall Street Journal]]|date=23 April 2015|first=Dion|last=Nissenbaum|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=13 September 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240913010800/https://www.wsj.com/articles/iranian-ships-turn-back-after-a-warning-about-arming-rebels-in-yemen-1429818885|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Ground clashes ===
Sudan said it was stationing ground troops in Saudi Arabia.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.ynetnews.com/articles/0,7340,L-4641287,00.html|work=[[Ynetnews]]|title=Sudan says will take part in Yemen campaign with ground, air forces|date=26 March 2015|access-date=31 March 2015|archive-date=2 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150402131212/http://www.ynetnews.com/articles/0,7340,L-4641287,00.html|url-status=live}}</ref> The Special Forces of the [[Bahrain Defence Force]], Taskforce 11, were also deployed to Yemen.<ref>{{cite news |publisher=[[Bahrain News Agency]] |title=HRH Crown Prince visits Royal Guard, honours servicemen who participated in Taskforce 11 |date=21 June 2017 |url=http://www.bna.bh/portal/en/news/791025 |access-date=13 July 2017 |quote=The Crown Prince presented the military medals awarded by His Majesty the King to the officers, non-commissioned officers, and personnel of the BDF's Special Operations Force (Taskforce 11) in recognition of their dedicated service in Yemen. |archive-date=14 July 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170714054355/http://www.bna.bh/portal/en/news/791025 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Between 31 March and April, Saudi and Houthi forces reportedly traded artillery and rocket fire across the border between SA and Yemen.<ref name="dozens"/><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-idUSKBN0MR0HE20150331|publisher=[[Reuters]]|title=Heavy clashes on Saudi-Yemeni border; Hadi government pleads for troops|date=31 March 2015|access-date=31 March 2015|archive-date=31 March 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150331070107/http://www.reuters.com/article/2015/03/31/us-yemen-security-idUSKBN0MR0HE20150331|url-status=live}}</ref> A Saudi border guard was killed on 2 April, the campaign's first confirmed coalition casualty.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-32157994|publisher=BBC News|title=Yemen crisis: Rebels storm presidential palace in Aden|date=2 April 2015|access-date=2 April 2015|archive-date=4 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150404213717/http://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-32157994|url-status=live}}</ref> Followed by another two soldiers killed the next day.<ref name="provinces">{{cite news|url=http://www.middleeasteye.net/news/yemeni-troops-retake-other-provinces-al-qaeda-rules-mukalla-port-city-2129445977|publisher=[[Middle East Eye]]|title=Yemeni troops retake provinces as al-Qaeda captures port city of Mukalla|date=4 April 2015|access-date=4 April 2015|archive-date=5 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150405164138/http://www.middleeasteye.net/news/yemeni-troops-retake-other-provinces-al-qaeda-rules-mukalla-port-city-2129445977|url-status=live}}</ref> An Egyptian truck driver was killed by Houthi shelling.<ref>{{cite news|title=Egyptian truck driver killed by shelling at Yemeni-Saudi border|url=http://english.ahram.org.eg/NewsContent/1/64/126826/Egypt/Politics-/Egyptian-truck-driver-killed-by-shelling-at-Yemeni.aspx|work=[[Al-Ahram]]|date=3 April 2015|access-date=3 April 2015|archive-date=7 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150407073632/http://english.ahram.org.eg/NewsContent/1/64/126826/Egypt/Politics-/Egyptian-truck-driver-killed-by-shelling-at-Yemeni.aspx|url-status=live}}</ref>

SA reportedly began removing sections of the [[Saudi–Yemen barrier]] fence along its border with the Saada and [[Hajjah Governorate|Hajjah]] [[governorates of Yemen|governorates]] on 3 April. The purpose of the removal was not immediately clear.<ref name="remove">{{cite news|url=http://www.aljazeera.com/news/2015/04/yemen-leader-loyalists-drive-houthis-aden-150403132431234.html|publisher=[[Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]]|title=Saudi-led airstrikes drive Houthis from Aden|date=3 April 2015|access-date=3 April 2015|archive-date=7 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150407002929/http://www.aljazeera.com/news/2015/04/yemen-leader-loyalists-drive-houthis-aden-150403132431234.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 12 April, members of the Takhya tribe launched an attack on a Saudi base after several of its members died in an airstrike. Weapons and ammunition were taken.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.yemenakhbar.com/yemen-news/166853.html?source=true|title=القبائل اليمنية تواصل إحكام سيطرتها على مواقع عسكرية سعودية وأسر 17 جندي سعودي – اخبار اليمن الان|access-date=26 April 2015|archive-date=27 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150427142938/http://www.yemenakhbar.com/yemen-news/166853.html?source=true|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.khabaragency.net/news24336.html|title=مصادر "خبر": القبائل اليمنية أسرت 17 ضابطاً وجندياً سعودياً|date=12 April 2015 |publisher=وكالة خبر للأنباء|access-date=26 April 2015|archive-date=27 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150427143645/http://www.khabaragency.net/news24336.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |work=أخبار متجددة |date=13 April 2015 |url=http://www.mtgdnews.com/arab/27743.html |script-title=ar:أخبار الصراع اليمني السعودي: قبائل "طخية" تأسر 17 ضابط وجندي سعودي وتقتل 20 آخرين|publisher=أخبار متجددة|access-date=26 April 2015|archive-date=27 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150427111030/http://www.mtgdnews.com/arab/27743.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 19 April, as Houthi leader Abdul-Malek El-Houthi accused SA of planning to invade Yemen,<ref name="Reuters">{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-saudi-idUSKBN0NA0JB20150419|title=Yemen's Houthi leader accuses Saudi Arabia of seeking to invade|date=19 April 2015|publisher=[[Reuters]]|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=24 September 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150924215442/http://www.reuters.com/article/2015/04/19/us-yemen-security-saudi-idUSKBN0NA0JB20150419|url-status=live}}</ref> Asiri claimed that coalition forces had information regarding a planned Houthi incursion into SA.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.spa.gov.sa/english/details.php?id=1351622|title=Daily Briefing of GCC Coalition spokesman Brig. Gen Ahmed Asiri|publisher=SPA|date=19 April 2015|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=27 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150427143229/http://www.spa.gov.sa/english/details.php?id=1351622|url-status=live}}</ref>
A Saudi border guard died on 19 April and two others were injured from gunfire and mortar shelling across the border.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.chicagotribune.com/news/nationworld/chi-yemen-navy-warship-iran-20150420-story.html#page=2|newspaper=[[Chicago Tribune]]|title=U.S. warship heads to Yemeni waters to intercept Iranian weapons|date=20 April 2015|access-date=21 April 2015|archive-date=26 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150426051447/http://www.chicagotribune.com/news/nationworld/chi-yemen-navy-warship-iran-20150420-story.html#page=2|url-status=live}}</ref>

== Operation Restoring Hope ==
[[File:Secretary Kerry Sits With Saudi King Salman, Foreign Minister al-Jubeir Before Bilateral Meeting in Washington (21148062001).jpg|thumb|King [[Salman of Saudi Arabia]] and Saudi Foreign Minister Adel al-Jubeir meet with [[John Kerry|U.S. Secretary of State John Kerry]] in September 2015.]]

On 21 April 2015, the [[Ministry of Defence (Saudi Arabia)|Saudi Defence Ministry]] declared it was ending the campaign of airstrikes because it had "successfully eliminated the threat" to its security posed by Houthi ballistic and heavy weaponry.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2015-04-21/saudi-arabia-says-airstrikes-in-yemen-succeeded-in-ending-threat|title=Saudi Arabia Says Airstrikes Succeeded in Ending Threat|first=Nadeem|last=Hamid|date=21 April 2015|publisher=[[Bloomberg News]]|access-date=21 April 2015|archive-date=19 April 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190419024024/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2015-04-21/saudi-arabia-says-airstrikes-in-yemen-succeeded-in-ending-threat|url-status=live}}</ref> It announced the start of a new phase codenamed Operation Restoring Hope.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.operationrenewalofhope.com/operation-decisive-storm-ends-operation-renewal-of-hope-begins/|title=With military objectives achieved, focus shifts to the political process|date=22 April 2015|publisher=Operation Renewal of Hope|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=5 February 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160205030438/http://www.operationrenewalofhope.com/operation-decisive-storm-ends-operation-renewal-of-hope-begins/|url-status=live}}</ref> In a televised address, Hadi said the end of airstrikes had come at his request and thanked the Arab coalition for their support.<ref name="bbc.com: Iran urges aid effort">{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-32408312 |title=Yemen conflict: Iran urges aid effort as Saudi air strikes end |date=22 April 2015 |publisher=BBC News |access-date=13 September 2024 |archive-date=8 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240808032902/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-32408312 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Earlier that day King Salman ordered the Saudi National Guard to join the military operation.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.cbsnews.com/news/saudi-arabia-king-salman-national-guard-yemen-iran-houthi-rebels/|title=Saudi king orders troops to join Yemen|publisher=[[CBS News]]|date=21 April 2015|access-date=21 April 2015|archive-date=23 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150423210731/http://www.cbsnews.com/news/saudi-arabia-king-salman-national-guard-yemen-iran-houthi-rebels/|url-status=live}}</ref> Air and naval strikes continued despite the announcement that ''Decisive Storm'' had ended.

Both the Omani<ref name="omanpeace">{{cite news |url=http://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-yemen-security-idUKKBN0ND0G620150422|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160124154016/http://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-yemen-security-idUKKBN0ND0G620150422|archive-date=24 January 2016|title=Yemen rivals battle on despite declared end to Saudi raids|publisher=[[Reuters]]|date=22 April 2015|access-date=22 April 2015}}</ref> and Iranian<ref name="bbc.com: Iran urges aid effort"/><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2015/apr/21/saudi-arabia-airstrikes-yemen-military-campaign-end|title=Saudi Arabia declares end to Yemen air strikes after four weeks of bombing|date=22 April 2015|work=[[The Guardian]]|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=13 September 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240913004600/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2015/apr/21/saudi-arabia-airstrikes-yemen-military-campaign-end|url-status=live}}</ref> governments said they welcomed the end of airstrikes. On 22 April, Oman presented a seven-point peace deal to both parties. The proposed peace treaty entailed the reinstatement of Hadi's government and the evacuation of Houthi fighters from major cities.<ref name="omanpeace"/>

'''Outside intervention'''

On 8 May, Saudi Arabia announced a five-day ceasefire set to start on 12 May,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2015/05/09/world/middleeast/saudi-arabia-announces-cease-fire-in-yemen.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150508215727/http://www.nytimes.com/2015/05/09/world/middleeast/saudi-arabia-announces-cease-fire-in-yemen.html |archive-date=2015-05-08 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |title=Saudi Arabia Announces Cease-Fire in Yemen |newspaper=[[The New York Times]] |date=8 May 2015 |first1=Alissa J. |last1=Rubin |author-link=Alissa J. Rubin |first2=Kareem |last2=Fahim}}</ref> following heavy pressure from the US.<ref>{{cite news|title=The war in Yemen: From Aden to Camp David|url=https://www.economist.com/news/middle-east-and-africa/21651273-could-temporary-truce-yemen-help-end-fighting-aden-camp-david|newspaper=[[The Economist]]|date=14 May 2015|access-date=18 May 2015|archive-date=17 May 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150517090626/http://www.economist.com/news/middle-east-and-africa/21651273-could-temporary-truce-yemen-help-end-fighting-aden-camp-david|url-status=live}}</ref> Later in the day, Saudi airplanes dropped leaflets in the [[Saada Governorate]] warning of airstrikes throughout the area.<ref>{{cite news|title=Arab coalition warns Yemenis to leave Saada province|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2015/05/yemenis-ordered-leave-saada-province-150508145754277.html|publisher=[[Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]]|date=9 May 2015|access-date=6 April 2020|archive-date=7 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200407061803/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2015/05/yemenis-ordered-leave-saada-province-150508145754277.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Houthi spokesman Mohamed al-Bukhaiti later told the [[BBC]] that the ceasefire had not been formally proposed and the Houthis would not respond until a plan was properly laid out.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-32677260|title=Yemen conflict: UN criticises Saudi civilian bombings|publisher=BBC News|access-date=11 May 2015|archive-date=13 September 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240913010934/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-32677260|url-status=live}}</ref> A spokesman for the Houthi-aligned military announced agreement to the ceasefire plan on 10 May, although he warned that a breach of the truce would prompt a military response.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/yemens-houthi-rebels-accept-five-day-truce-proposal-1431250255|newspaper=[[The Wall Street Journal]]|title=Yemen's Houthi Rebels Accept Five-Day Truce Proposal|date=10 May 2015|first1=Asa|last1=Fitch|first2=Mohammed|last2=al-Kibsi|access-date=10 May 2015|archive-date=11 May 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150511134536/http://www.wsj.com/articles/yemens-houthi-rebels-accept-five-day-truce-proposal-1431250255|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 13 May, humanitarian agencies said they were trying to get aid into Yemen after a five-day ceasefire took effect on Tuesday night. Ships carrying humanitarian supplies docked at the Houthi-controlled [[Red Sea]] port of [[Hodeidah]] as planes were standing by to help evacuate the injured.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-32719194 |title=Yemen conflict: Aid effort begins as truce takes hold |date=13 May 2015 |publisher=BBC News |access-date=13 September 2024 |archive-date=15 May 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150515234606/http://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-32719194 |url-status=live }}</ref> Meanwhile, King Salman doubled his country's Yemen aid pledge to $540&nbsp;million, funds the UN said would "meet the life-saving and protection needs of 7.5&nbsp;million people affected".<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.yahoo.com/saudi-king-doubles-yemen-aid-pledge-540-mn-104046759.html |title=Saudi king doubles Yemen aid pledge to $540 mn |date=13 May 2015 |work=[[Yahoo! News]] |agency=Agence France-Presse |access-date=13 September 2024 |archive-date=23 January 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160123031953/http://news.yahoo.com/saudi-king-doubles-yemen-aid-pledge-540-mn-104046759.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Airstrikes ===
At the operation's announcement, coalition leadership stressed that their campaign would attempt a political solution and that they would continue the air and naval blockade.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.dailystar.com.lb/News/Middle-East/2015/Apr-21/295278-saudi-led-coalition-ends-military-operation-in-yemen.ashx|title=Saudi-led coalition ends military operation in Yemen |website=Daily Star |access-date=28 February 2023|archive-date=30 December 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181230041644/http://www.dailystar.com.lb/News/Middle-East/2015/Apr-21/295278-saudi-led-coalition-ends-military-operation-in-yemen.ashx}}</ref> Airstrikes resumed almost immediately following the coalition's announcement of the end of Operation Decisive Storm.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-32430003 |title=Yemen conflict: Saudis launch new air strikes on rebels |publisher=BBC News |date=23 April 2015 |access-date=13 September 2024 |archive-date=6 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240706181610/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-32430003 |url-status=live }}</ref>

On 22 April airstrikes continued in [[Taiz]], where an army base was hit shortly after Houthi fighters took it over,<ref name="npr-shifts">{{cite news|url=https://www.npr.org/blogs/thetwo-way/2015/04/22/401453167/saudi-arabia-shifts-military-campaign-in-yemen-airstrikes-continue|publisher=[[NPR]]|title=Saudi Arabia Shifts Military Campaign in Yemen; Airstrikes Continue|date=22 April 2015|first=Bill|last=Chappell|access-date=22 April 2015|archive-date=23 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150423131631/http://www.npr.org/blogs/thetwo-way/2015/04/22/401453167/saudi-arabia-shifts-military-campaign-in-yemen-airstrikes-continue|url-status=live}}</ref> and Aden, where an airstrike targeted Houthi tanks moving into a contested district,<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2015/apr/22/yemen-crisis-air-strike-hits-aden-after-saudi-arabia-ends-bombing-campaign|newspaper=[[The Guardian]]|title=Yemen crisis: air strike hits Aden after Saudi Arabia ends bombing campaign|first=Ian|last=Black|author-link=Ian Black (journalist)|date=22 April 2015|access-date=22 April 2015|archive-date=6 December 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171206135814/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2015/apr/22/yemen-crisis-air-strike-hits-aden-after-saudi-arabia-ends-bombing-campaign|url-status=live}}</ref> among other locations, such as Hodeidah and [[Ibb]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-idUSKBN0NC2LD20150423|publisher=[[Reuters]]|title=Saudi-led coalition launches air strikes throughout Yemen: residents|date=23 April 2015|access-date=23 April 2015|archive-date=23 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150423053811/http://www.reuters.com/article/2015/04/23/us-yemen-security-idUSKBN0NC2LD20150423|url-status=live}}</ref> The Houthis continued to fight for territory,<ref name="npr-shifts"/> with a Houthi spokesman saying the group would be prepared for peace talks on the condition of "a complete halt of attacks". The previous round of UN-sponsored talks collapsed after Houthi rebels attacked Hadi's residence in Sanaa.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.cnn.com/2015/04/22/middleeast/yemen-crisis/|title=Houthis call for peace talks as Saudi planes strike Yemen|date=22 April 2015|work=CNN|access-date=22 April 2015|archive-date=3 January 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190103105455/https://www.cnn.com/2015/04/22/middleeast/yemen-crisis/|url-status=live}}</ref>

By 26 April, coalition forces were striking what they described as Houthi military targets in Sanaa and Aden and in other locations, notably in Sa'ada province near the Saudi border, nearly every night.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.aljazeera.com/news/middleeast/2015/04/air-raids-ground-clashes-rage-yemen-150426123231533.html|title=Air raids and ground clashes rage in Yemen|date=26 April 2015|publisher=[[Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]]|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=3 May 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150503060253/http://www.aljazeera.com/news/middleeast/2015/04/air-raids-ground-clashes-rage-yemen-150426123231533.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-strikes-idUSKBN0NH04C20150426|title=Fighting escalates across Yemen, air strikes on capital Sanaa|date=26 April 2015|publisher=[[Reuters]]}}</ref> On 26 April, after midnight, airstrikes struck Houthi and pro-Saleh positions and targets in and around Sanaa, Aden, and the [[Marib Governorate|Marib]] and [[Ad Dali' Governorate|Ad Dali']] governorates, backing up anti-Houthi fighters in the latter three locations, with more than 90 rebels reportedly killed.<ref>{{cite news|title=Saudi air raids strike Yemeni capital|url=http://www.3news.co.nz/world/saudi-air-raids-strike-yemeni-capital-2015042708|agency=Agence France-Presse|publisher=[[MediaWorks New Zealand]]|date=27 April 2015|access-date=27 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150518082725/http://www.3news.co.nz/world/saudi-air-raids-strike-yemeni-capital-2015042708|archive-date=18 May 2015}}</ref> Coalition warships shelled fighters near Aden's commercial port. Saudi warplanes also targeted Houthis in the Saada Governorate, while Saudi artillery fired on targets in the Hajjah Governorate along the border.<ref>{{cite news |title=Air raids and ground clashes rage in Yemen |date=26 April 2015 |url=http://www.aljazeera.com/news/middleeast/2015/04/air-raids-ground-clashes-rage-yemen-150426123231533.html |publisher=[[Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]] |access-date=26 April 2015 |archive-date=3 May 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150503060253/http://www.aljazeera.com/news/middleeast/2015/04/air-raids-ground-clashes-rage-yemen-150426123231533.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The Saudi National Guard was deployed on the border.<ref>{{cite news|title=First Saudi National Guards reach Yemen border zone|url=http://english.ahram.org.eg/NewsContent/2/8/128747/World/Region/First-Saudi-National-Guards-reach-Yemen-border-zon.aspx|agency=Agence France-Presse|newspaper=[[Al-Ahram]]|date=27 April 2015|access-date=27 April 2015|archive-date=29 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150429153045/http://english.ahram.org.eg/NewsContent/2/8/128747/World/Region/First-Saudi-National-Guards-reach-Yemen-border-zon.aspx|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 28 April, [[Sanaa International Airport]] was bombed<ref>{{cite news|url=https://abcnews.go.com/International/wireStory/yemen-rebels-allies-advance-southern-city-aden-30671835|publisher=[[ABC News (United States)|ABC News]]|title=Yemen Rebels and Allies Advance in Southern City of Aden|first=Ahmed|last=Al-Haj|date=29 April 2015|access-date=29 April 2015|archive-date=1 May 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150501232331/http://abcnews.go.com/International/wireStory/yemen-rebels-allies-advance-southern-city-aden-30671835|url-status=live}}</ref> by Saudi F-15 fighters to prevent an Iranian plane<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/news/picture/yemen-airport-bombed?articleId=USRTX1ATMJ|publisher=[[Reuters]]|title=Yemen airport bombed|date=29 April 2015|access-date=29 April 2015|archive-date=1 May 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150501213644/http://www.reuters.com/news/picture/yemen-airport-bombed?articleId=USRTX1ATMJ|url-status=live}}</ref> belonging to [[Iranian Red Crescent Society]] (IRCS) from landing, while it was approaching to land. The fighters had warned the plane to turn back, in an unsuccessful attempt to thwart its landing, but the Iranian pilot ignored the "illegal warnings", saying that, on the basis of international law, his plane did not need further permission to land.<ref>{{cite news|title=Aid flights to Yemen blocked after Saudi Arabian jets bomb airport runway|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2015/apr/28/aid-flights-to-yemen-blocked-after-saudi-arabia-bombs-airport-runway|access-date=30 April 2015|newspaper=[[The Guardian]]|agency=[[Reuters]]|date=29 April 2015|archive-date=12 June 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180612162945/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2015/apr/28/aid-flights-to-yemen-blocked-after-saudi-arabia-bombs-airport-runway|url-status=live}}</ref>

On the night of 6 May 2015, the Saudi-led coalition carried out 130 airstrikes in Yemen in a 24-hour period. At first, coalition spokesperson Ahmed Asiri admitted that schools and hospitals were targeted but claimed that these were used as weapon storage sites. Asiri later claimed that his words had been mistranslated. The United Nations humanitarian coordinator for Yemen Johannes Van Der Klaauw said that these bombings constituted a [[war crime]]. "The [[Indiscriminate attack|indiscriminate bombing]] of populated areas, with or without prior warning, is a contravention international humanitarian law," he said. He continued to say that he was particularly concerned about airstrikes on Saada "where scores of civilians were reportedly killed and thousands were forced to flee their homes after the coalition declared the entire governate a military target".<ref name="edition.cnn.com">{{cite news |title=U.N. rep accuses Saudi-led coalition of violating international law |author=Mariano Castillo |date=12 May 2015 |work=CNN|url=https://edition.cnn.com/2015/05/09/asia/saudi-airstrikes-yemen/ |author-link=Mariano Castillo |access-date=13 September 2024 |archive-date=15 April 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240415112924/https://edition.cnn.com/2015/05/09/asia/saudi-airstrikes-yemen |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[File:Secretary Kerry Listens As Saudi Foreign Minister al-Jubeir Announces During Paris News Conference That Yemen Cease Fire Will Begin Tuesday (17427072602).jpg|thumb|Saudi foreign minister [[Adel al-Jubeir]] announced a five-day ceasefire in Yemen, 8 May 2015.]]

The [[Iranian Foreign Ministry]] summoned the Saudi [[chargé d'affaires]], and the [[Iranian Parliament]] and the Iranian Red Crescent Society blasted Saudi Arabia for blocking Iranian humanitarian aid.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://en.ghatreh.com/view/5043/Iranian-Parliament-Condemns-Saudi-Arabia-for-Blocking-Humanitarian-Aid-to-Yemen|title=Iranian Parliament Condemns Saudi Arabia for Blocking Humanitarian Aid to Yemen|work=ghatreh |access-date=18 May 2015|archive-date=18 May 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150518100731/http://en.ghatreh.com/view/5043/Iranian-Parliament-Condemns-Saudi-Arabia-for-Blocking-Humanitarian-Aid-to-Yemen|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://kayhan.ir/en/news/13138/iran-summons-saudi-envoy-over-blocking-humanitarian-aid-to-yemen|title=Iran Summons Saudi Envoy Over Blocking Humanitarian Aid to Yemen|newspaper=[[Kayhan]]|access-date=18 May 2015|archive-date=18 May 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150518094822/http://kayhan.ir/en/news/13138/iran-summons-saudi-envoy-over-blocking-humanitarian-aid-to-yemen|url-status=live}}</ref>

The UN [[Office for the Coordination of Humanitarian Affairs]] (OCHA) "strongly urged" the coalition to stop targeting airports and seaports so that aid could reach all Yemenis.<ref name="reuters-airport"/><ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.yementimes.com/en/1875/news/5077/Humanitarian-flights-unable-to-land-after-bombing-Sana'a-International-Airport.htm|title=Humanitarian flights unable to land, after bombing Sana'a International Airport|work=[[Yemen Times]]}}</ref>

ICRC and [[Médecins Sans Frontières]] (MSF), also known as Doctors Without Borders, said that they were extremely concerned about damage to the airports at Sanaa and to the port city of [[Hodeidah]].<ref name="reuters-airport">{{cite news |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-humanitarian/un-urges-saudi-led-coalition-to-stop-targeting-yemen-airport-idUSKBN0NP1VJ20150504 |title=UN urges Saudi-led coalition to stop targeting Yemen airport |publisher=[[Reuters]] |date=4 May 2015 |access-date=13 July 2015 |archive-date=8 September 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170908172544/http://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-humanitarian/un-urges-saudi-led-coalition-to-stop-targeting-yemen-airport-idUSKBN0NP1VJ20150504 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Overnight on 29 and 30 April, Saudi Arabia was reported to have airdropped arms to anti-Houthi fighters in Taiz.<ref name="ReferenceB">{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-idUSKBN0NK0N020150429|title=Heavy fighting in Yemen, Saudi Arabia trains tribal fighters|date=30 April 2015|publisher=[[Reuters]]|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=22 April 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230422233021/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-idUSKBN0NK0N020150429|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 30 April, airstrikes hit five provinces.<ref name="ReferenceB"/> New airstrikes hit SIA, completely halting aid deliveries.<ref name="ReferenceA">{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-idUSKBN0NL0Q720150430|title=Houthis clash with Saudi forces on Yemen border, dozens killed|publisher=[[Reuters]]|date=30 April 2015|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=18 April 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230418130221/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-idUSKBN0NL0Q720150430|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[File:Air strike in Sana'a 11 May 2015 01.jpg|thumb|upright|An airstrike in Sanaa, 11 May 2015]]

On 6 May, coalition airstrikes targeted the Police Training Center in the [[Dhamar Governorate]], damaging nearby houses<ref name="Saba Net – Yemen news agency">{{cite news |date=6 May 2015 |url=http://www.sabanews.net/en/news395227.htm |title=Saudi airstrikes kill 11 people in Dhamar |publisher=[[Saba News Agency]] |access-date=11 May 2015 |archive-date=10 May 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150510174017/http://www.sabanews.net/en/news395227.htm |url-status=live }}</ref> meanwhile the civil aviation authority announced it would re-open the airport to receive aid.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.saharasamay.com/world-news/676572666/yemen-saudi-led-warplanes-bomb-sanaa-airport.html|title=Yemen:Saudi-led warplanes bomb Sanaa airport|access-date=11 May 2015|archive-date=6 December 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171206074643/http://www.saharasamay.com/world-news/676572666/yemen-saudi-led-warplanes-bomb-sanaa-airport.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

Coalition airstrikes targeted the houses of Saleh in Sanaa in the early hours of 10 May, eyewitnesses said. Khabar, a Yemeni news agency allied with Saleh said that the former president and his family were unharmed.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2015/may/10/saudi-air-strikes-in-yemen-target-house-of-ex-president-saleh|title=Saudi air strikes in Yemen target house of ex-president Saleh|date=10 May 2015|work=[[The Guardian]]|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=19 November 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171119152551/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2015/may/10/saudi-air-strikes-in-yemen-target-house-of-ex-president-saleh|url-status=live}}</ref>

The Moroccan government said on 10 May that one of its [[General Dynamics F-16 Fighting Falcon]] aircraft taking part in the air campaign went missing in action over Yemen, along with its pilot.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://abcnews.go.com/International/wireStory/moroccan-fighter-jet-missing-yemen-30946660|publisher=[[ABC News (United States)|ABC News]]|title=Moroccan Fighter Jet Goes Missing in Yemen|date=11 May 2015|access-date=10 May 2015|archive-date=18 May 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150518093839/http://abcnews.go.com/International/wireStory/moroccan-fighter-jet-missing-yemen-30946660|url-status=live}}</ref> The Houthis claimed responsibility, with Yemeni state TV broadcasting a report on the jet being downed by tribal militias over the Saada Governorate and showing images of the wreckage.<ref name="airloss">{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-32688816|publisher=BBC News|title=Yemen conflict: Houthi rebels 'down Moroccan warplane'|date=11 May 2015|access-date=11 May 2015|archive-date=22 December 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171222113523/http://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-32688816|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 18 May, Saudi-led airstrikes reportedly resumed on Houthi positions after a humanitarian ceasefire expired late the previous day; three coalition airstrikes hit [[Saada]]. Yemen's exiled Foreign Minister Riyadh Yassin blamed the rebel group for the renewal of hostilities. Al-Arabiya said Saudi forces shelled Houthi outposts along Yemen's northern border after the fighters fired mortars at a Saudi army post in Najran province.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-idUSKBN0O30II20150518|title=Saudi-led air strikes hit Yemen after truce expires|date=18 May 2015|publisher=[[Reuters]]|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=23 April 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230423124400/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-idUSKBN0O30II20150518|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 23 May, OCHA reported that airstrikes continued in the northern governorates of Sa'ada (Baqim, Haydan, Saqayn and As Safra) and Hajjah (Abs, Hayran, Haradh, Huth, Kuhlan Affar and Sahar districts). The road connecting Haradh and Huth districts was reportedly hit. Airstrikes were also reported in Al Jawf Governorate (Bart Al Anan district).<ref>{{cite web |url=http://reliefweb.int/sites/reliefweb.int/files/resources/OCHA%20Yemen%20Flash%20Update%20on%20the%20Escalating%20Conflict%20-%20No.%2036%20-%2023%20May%202015.pdf |title=Yemen: Escalating Conflict Flash Update 36 |date=23 May 2015 |website=[[ReliefWeb]] |access-date=25 February 2016 |archive-date=25 May 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150525184147/http://reliefweb.int/sites/reliefweb.int/files/resources/OCHA%20Yemen%20Flash%20Update%20on%20the%20Escalating%20Conflict%20-%20No.%2036%20-%2023%20May%202015.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref>

On 27 May, airstrikes hit a police station in the capital, [[Sanaa]], killing 45 officers.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.thehansindia.com/posts/index/2015-05-28/Saudi-led-airstrikes-on-Yemen-police-headquarters-kills-45-153829|title=Saudi-led airstrikes on Yemen police headquarters kills 45|date=28 May 2015|website=thehansindia.com|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=28 May 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150528180345/http://www.thehansindia.com/posts/index/2015-05-28/Saudi-led-airstrikes-on-Yemen-police-headquarters-kills-45-153829|url-status=live}}</ref> The Houthi-controlled Ministry of Health announced that in total, 96 people were killed.

On 3 June, the residence of a Houthi leader in Ibb province was hit by an airstrike, according to eyewitnesses.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-strikes-idUSKBN0OJ0J520150603|title=Air strikes kill 20 Houthi fighters in Yemen's Aden: militia|date=3 June 2015|publisher=[[Reuters]]|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=22 April 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230422200537/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-strikes-idUSKBN0OJ0J520150603|url-status=live}}</ref>
