On 12 June, Saudi jets bombed the [[UNESCO World Heritage Site]] of Sanaa Old City, killing at least six people and destroying some of the ancient buildings. [[UNESCO]] Director General [[Irina Bokova]] said in a statement that she is "profoundly distressed by the loss of human lives as well as by damage inflicted on one of the world's oldest jewels of Islamic urban landscape". Locals also condemned the action.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.euronews.com/2015/06/12/unesco-condemns-bombing-of-sanaa-s-old-city/|title=UNESCO condemns bombing of Sanaa's Old City|publisher=Euronews|access-date=13 July 2015|archive-date=14 June 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150614093836/http://www.euronews.com/2015/06/12/unesco-condemns-bombing-of-sanaa-s-old-city/|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 23 September 2015, the Saudi-led coalition destroyed a ceramics factory in the town of Matnah. One civilian was killed and others were wounded. According to the BBC, the bomb is believed to have been produced in the United Kingdom by GEC-Marconi Dynamics.<ref>{{cite AV media |title=UK-made bomb 'hit Yemen factory' |author=[[Orla Guerin]] |date=25 March 2016 |publisher=BBC News |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/av/world-middle-east-35898999/uk-made-bomb-hit-yemen-factory |access-date=7 May 2017 |archive-date=13 September 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240913004704/https://www.bbc.com/news/av/world-middle-east-35898999 |url-status=live }}</ref> The factory's owner Ghalib al-Sawary told the BBC: "We built it over 20 years but to destroy it took only twenty minutes."<ref name="auto2"/> Campaigners say this attack was a violation of the laws of war.

On 26 October 2015 Médecins Sans Frontières reported that a coalition airstrike had completely destroyed a hospital they ran in Saada province's Haydan governorate, including the operating room. When the first strike hit an unused part of the hospital the facility was completely evacuated, so there were no direct casualties. A spokesman for the coalition forces, Brig-Gen [[Ahmed Asiri|Ahmed al-Asiri]], denied responsibility for the attack.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-34645469|title=Yemen conflict: MSF hospital destroyed by air strikes|publisher=BBC News|access-date=15 November 2015|archive-date=4 May 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190504093539/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-34645469|url-status=live}}</ref> "With the hospital destroyed, at least 200,000 people now have no access to lifesaving medical care," MSF said. "This attack is another illustration of a complete disregard for civilians in Yemen, where bombings have become a daily routine," said Hassan Boucenine, MSF head of mission in Yemen. The GPS coordinates of the only hospital in the Haydan district were regularly shared with the Saudi-led coalition, and the roof of the facility was clearly identified with the MSF logo, he said.<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20151117032959/http://news.xinhuanet.com/english/2015-10/28/c_134756413.htm "MSF hospital in Yemen bombed by airstrike"], Xinhua (28 October 2015)</ref> UNICEF said the hospital in Saada was the 39th health center hit in Yemen since March, when the violence escalated. "More children in Yemen may well die from a lack of medicines and healthcare than from bullets and bombs," its executive director [[Anthony Lake]] said in a statement. He added that critical shortages of fuel, medication, electricity and water could mean many more will close. [[Amnesty International]] said the strike may amount to a war crime and called for an independent investigation.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-idUSKCN0SL0VK20151027|title=Yemeni MSF hospital bombed, Saudi-led coalition denies responsibility|publisher=[[Reuters]]|access-date=15 November 2015|archive-date=9 December 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151209025234/http://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-idUSKCN0SL0VK20151027|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.foxnews.com/world/doctors-without-borders-says-saudi-led-airstrikes-bomb-yemen-hospital/|title=Doctors Without Borders says Saudi-led airstrikes bomb Yemen hospital|publisher=Fox News|access-date=15 November 2015|archive-date=17 November 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151117055441/http://www.foxnews.com/world/2015/10/28/doctors-without-borders-says-saudi-led-airstrikes-bomb-yemen-hospital/|url-status=live}}</ref>

In February 2016, the Saudis bombed the ancient citadel of Kawkaban, killing seven civilians.<ref name="auto2">{{cite news |title=A year of war that has set Yemen back decades |author=Orla Guerin |publisher=BBC News |date=26 March 2016 |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-35901321 |author-link=Orla Guerin |access-date=13 September 2024 |archive-date=5 September 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240905220950/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-35901321 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 8 October 2016, Saudi-led airstrikes [[Sanaa funeral airstrike|targeted]] a hall in Sanaa where a funeral was taking place. At least 140 people were killed and about 600 were wounded. According to ''The Independent'', one rescuer said: "The place has been turned into a lake of blood."<ref name="independent.co.uk">{{cite news |title=Saudi-led coalition in Yemen accused of 'genocide' after airstrike on funeral hall kills 140; US says it will 'review' its support after latest attack |author=Ben Kentish |date=9 October 2016 |newspaper=[[The Independent]] |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/middle-east/yemen-air-strike-bomb-kills-140-saudi-arabia-usa-white-house-a7352386.html |access-date=13 September 2024 |archive-date=31 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220531144447/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/middle-east/yemen-air-strike-bomb-kills-140-saudi-arabia-usa-white-house-a7352386.html |url-status=live }}</ref> After initially denying it was behind the attack, the Coalition's Joint Incidents Assessment Team admitted that it had bombed the hall but claimed that this attack had been a mistake caused by bad information.<ref>{{cite news |date=15 October 2016 |title=Saudi-led coalition admits to bombing Yemen funeral |newspaper=[[The Guardian]] |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2016/oct/15/saudi-led-coalition-admits-to-bombing-yemen-funeral |access-date=13 September 2024 |archive-date=31 January 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170131064759/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2016/oct/15/saudi-led-coalition-admits-to-bombing-yemen-funeral |url-status=live }}</ref> After this attack, US national security spokesperson said that the US government was "deeply disturbed" by the bombing and added that US support for the Saudi-led coalition was "not a blank cheque". He added "we have initiated an immediate review of our already significantly reduced support to the Saudi-led Coalition."<ref name="independent.co.uk"/> The United Nations humanitarian co-ordinator in Yemen Jamie McGoldrick said he was "shocked and outraged" by the "horrific" bombing. "This violence against civilians in Yemen must stop," he said.<ref name="independent.co.uk"/>

On the night of 15 February 2017, the Saudi-led coalition bombed a funeral reception near Sanaa. Initial reports suggest the bombing killed nine women and one child with ten more women reported wounded. "People heard the sound of planes and started running from the house but then the bombs hit the house directly. The roof collapsed and there was blood everywhere," a resident of the village told a Reuters news agency cameraman.<ref>{{cite news |date=16 February 2017 |title=Air raid on Yemen funeral 'kills at least nine women'; Houthi rebels blame Arab coalition for attack on Shiraa village 40km north of capital Sanaa |publisher=[[Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]] |url=http://www.aljazeera.com/news/2017/02/air-raid-yemen-funeral-kills-women-170216093707979.html |access-date=13 September 2024 |archive-date=7 October 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191007085031/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2017/02/air-raid-yemen-funeral-kills-women-170216093707979.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

An explosion in a warehouse on Sunday 7 April 2019, in Sanaa, have killed at least 11 civilians, including school children and left more than 39 people wounded. The [[Associated Press]] news agency said 13 killed, including 7 children and more than 100 were wounded. According to Al Jazeera and Houthi officials, the civilians were killed in a Saudi-led coalition airstrike.<ref>{{cite news |title=Saudi-led air raids 'kill at least 11 civilians' in Yemen's Sanaa |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2019/04/saudi-led-air-raids-kill-11-civilians-yemen-sanaa-190407201257046.html |access-date=8 April 2019 |publisher=[[Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]] |date=7 April 2019 |archive-date=8 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190408030652/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2019/04/saudi-led-air-raids-kill-11-civilians-yemen-sanaa-190407201257046.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The Saudi-led coalition denied any airstrikes took place that day on Sanaa. The state-run news agency in Aden, aligned with the internationally recognized government, said the rebels had stored weapons at the warehouse. According to ''[[The Washington Post]]'', "some families and residents of the district of Sawan said the explosion occurred after a fire erupted inside the warehouse. They said a fire sent columns of white smoke rising into the air, followed by the explosion." Their accounts were confirmed by several videos filmed by bystanders.<ref>{{cite news |title=Explosion in Yemen Warehouse Kills at Least 13, Including 7 Children |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/07/world/middleeast/yemen-explosion-warehouse.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190407222610/https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/07/world/middleeast/yemen-explosion-warehouse.html |archive-date=2019-04-07 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |access-date=8 April 2019 |newspaper=[[The New York Times]] |date=7 April 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/middle_east/un-decries-killing-of-7-children-in-yemen-explosion/2019/04/08/100e26f8-5a00-11e9-98d4-844088d135f2_story.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190409004122/https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/middle_east/un-decries-killing-of-7-children-in-yemen-explosion/2019/04/08/100e26f8-5a00-11e9-98d4-844088d135f2_story.html|archive-date=9 April 2019|title=Yemenis recount horror of mysterious blast in capital|author=The Washington Post|date=8 April 2019}}</ref>

