=== Ground combat ===
{{See also|Battle of Aden (2015)}}
On 3 April 2015, [[CNN]] cited an unnamed Saudi source who claimed that Saudi special forces were on the ground in and around [[Aden]], "coordinating and guiding" the resistance.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.cnn.com/2015/04/03/middleeast/yemen-saudi-forces/index.html|work=CNN|title=Saudi special forces help oppose Houthi rebels in Yemen, source says|date=3 April 2015|access-date=3 April 2015|archive-date=4 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150404055228/http://www.cnn.com/2015/04/03/middleeast/yemen-saudi-forces/index.html|url-status=live}}</ref> The Saudi government officially declined to comment on whether it had [[special forces]], with Saudi Ambassador to the United States [[Adel al-Jubeir]] saying on 2 April that Saudi Arabia had no "formal" troops in Aden.<ref name="remove"/>

The Battle of Aden came to an end with pro-Hadi forces again seized control of Aden port and moving into the city's commercial center.<ref name="Mohammed Mukhashaf">{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-aden-idUSKCN0PP16I20150715|publisher=Reuters|title=Saudi-backed Yemen forces take Aden port from Houthis: residents|author=Mohammed Mukhashaf|date=16 July 2015|access-date=16 July 2015|archive-date=28 November 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151128192557/http://www.reuters.com/article/2015/07/15/us-yemen-security-aden-idUSKCN0PP16I20150715|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Glen Carey">{{cite news|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2015-07-16/saudi-backed-forces-in-yemen-battle-to-take-districts-in-aden|publisher=Bloomberg|title=Saudi-Backed Forces in Yemen Claim Victories in Aden Battle|author=Glen Carey|date=17 July 2015|access-date=17 July 2015|archive-date=3 April 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190403012851/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2015-07-16/saudi-backed-forces-in-yemen-battle-to-take-districts-in-aden|url-status=live}}</ref> On 22 July, pro-Hadi forces had retaken full control of Aden, and the Aden Airport was reopened. In late July, an offensive launched by pro-Hadi forces drove Houthi forces out of the towns neighboring Aden.<ref name="offensive2"/>

On 4 September a Houthi [[OTR-21 Tochka]] missile hit an ammunition dump at a military base in Safer in Ma'rib Governorate killing 52 UAE, 10 Saudi and 5 Bahraini soldiers. The Safer base was being built up by coalition forces for a push against Sanaa.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-34163982|title=Yemen crisis: UAE launches fresh Yemen attacks|publisher=BBC News|date=5 September 2015|access-date=6 September 2015|archive-date=7 September 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150907204725/http://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-34163982|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.thenational.ae/world/middle-east/marib-province-is-crucial-to-coalition-victory-in-yemen|title=Marib province is crucial to coalition victory in Yemen|work=The National|date=7 September 2015|access-date=7 September 2015|archive-date=13 September 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240913004605/https://www.thenationalnews.com/world/why-marib-province-is-crucial-to-coalition-victory-in-yemen-1.129405|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.thenational.ae/uae/emirati-serviceman-dies-during-yemen-ground-offensive|title=Emirati serviceman dies during Yemen ground offensive|work=The National|date=14 September 2015|access-date=14 September 2015|archive-date=15 September 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150915080209/http://www.thenational.ae/uae/emirati-serviceman-dies-during-yemen-ground-offensive|url-status=live}}</ref> "It was the deadliest single attack on coalition soldiers since the start of its operation against Houthi rebels in March" Asseri said.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://edition.cnn.com/2015/09/05/middleeast/yemen-weapons-depot-attack/|title=Saudi-led coalition strikes back at Houthis|last=Gray|first=Melissa|date=5 September 2015|work=CNN|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=6 February 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230206180103/http://edition.cnn.com/2015/09/05/middleeast/yemen-weapons-depot-attack/|url-status=live}}</ref> The attacked was the highest casualty loss in the history of the UAE military.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-idUSKCN0R507Y20150905|title=Coalition attacks Yemen capital after UAE, Saudi soldiers killed|publisher=[[Reuters]]|access-date=15 November 2015|archive-date=26 January 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160126074505/http://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-idUSKCN0R507Y20150905|url-status=live}}</ref> Qatar deployed 1000 troops to Yemen after the incident.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.aljazeera.com/news/2015/09/qatar-deploys-1000-ground-troops-fight-yemen-150907043020594.html|title=Qatar deploys 1,000 ground troops to fight in Yemen|author=[[Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]] and agencies|date=7 September 2015|publisher=[[Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]]|access-date=13 September 2024|archive-date=21 September 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200921003505/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2015/09/qatar-deploys-1000-ground-troops-fight-yemen-150907043020594.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

By 8 September it was reported that the Saudi-led forces deployed in Yemen exceeded 10,000 troops and included 30 [[Boeing AH-64 Apache|AH-64 Apache]] attack helicopters.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.arabianbusiness.com/number-of-saudi-led-coalition-troops-in-yemen-rises-10-000--605525.html|title=Number of Saudi-led coalition troops in Yemen 'rises to 10,000'|agency=[[Reuters]]|newspaper=[[Arabian Business]]|date=8 September 2015|access-date=9 September 2015|archive-date=9 September 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150909182732/http://www.arabianbusiness.com/number-of-saudi-led-coalition-troops-in-yemen-rises-10-000--605525.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 14 December media reported a Houthi & Saleh Forces [[December 2015 Taiz missile attack|missile attack at a Saudi military camp south-west of the besieged city of Taiz]],<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.alaraby.co.uk/english/news/2015/12/14/dozens-of-gulf-troops-dead-in-yemen-rocket-attack|title=Gulf troops dead in Yemen rocket attack, sources|website=alaraby.co.uk|date=14 December 2015|access-date=8 February 2016|archive-date=16 January 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160116191744/http://www.alaraby.co.uk/english/news/2015/12/14/dozens-of-gulf-troops-dead-in-yemen-rocket-attack|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-idUSKBN0TX0FW20151214|title=Two top Gulf commanders killed in Yemen rocket strike: sources|publisher=[[Reuters]]|date=14 December 2015|access-date=8 February 2016|archive-date=7 November 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171107014812/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-yemen-security-idUSKBN0TX0FW20151214|url-status=live}}</ref> while sources confirmed the killings of over 150 coalition soldiers including 23 Saudi troops, 9 UAE officers and soldiers, 7 Moroccan soldiers and 42 Blackwater troops.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.ibtimes.com/saudi-coalition-houthi-rebels-intensify-attacks-yemen-ahead-proposed-ceasefire-2223830|title=Saudi Coalition, Houthi Rebels Intensify Attacks in Yemen Ahead of Proposed Ceasefire|website=Financial News|date=14 December 2015|access-date=8 February 2016|archive-date=4 October 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161004172106/http://www.ibtimes.com/saudi-coalition-houthi-rebels-intensify-attacks-yemen-ahead-proposed-ceasefire-2223830|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.moroccoworldnews.com/2015/12/175077/seven-moroccan-soldiers-reportedly-killed-in-yemen/|title=even Moroccan Soldiers Reportedly Killed in Yemen|website=Morocco World News|date=14 December 2015|access-date=8 February 2016|archive-date=16 February 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160216225706/http://www.moroccoworldnews.com/2015/12/175077/seven-moroccan-soldiers-reportedly-killed-in-yemen/|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 19 December 2015, reported clashes leaves over 40 Houthi rebels and 35 government loyalists dead, with dozens wounded on both sides.<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20151220101004/http://time.com/4156068/fierce-fighting-in-yemen-kills-at-least-75/ "Fierce Fighting in Yemen kills at Least 75]. ''[[Time (magazine)|Time]]''.</ref>

In June 2018, anti-Houthi forces led by Saudi Arabia and the [[United Arab Emirates]] assaulted the port of [[Hodeidah]],<ref>"[https://www.nytimes.com/2018/06/13/world/middleeast/yemen-al-hudaydah-assault-saudi-coalition.html Arab Coalition Attacks Yemeni City in Bid to Oust Rebels] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240913010808/https://www.nytimes.com/2018/06/13/world/middleeast/yemen-al-hudaydah-assault-saudi-coalition.html |date=13 September 2024 }}". ''The New York Times''. 13 June 2018.</ref> in an effort to dislodge Houthi forces.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/u-s-deepens-role-in-yemen-fight-offers-gulf-allies-airstrike-target-assistance-1528830371 |url-access=subscription |newspaper=[[The Wall Street Journal]] |first=Dion |last=Nissenbam |title=U.S. Deepens Role in Yemen Fight, Offers Gulf Allies Airstrike-Target Assistance}}</ref>

In January 2022, Yemeni troops backed by the Saudi-led coalition took control of the entire [[Shabwah Governorate]] from the Houthis.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Yeranian |first=Edward |title=Saudi Coalition, Yemeni Allies Capture Energy-Rich Shabwa Province From Houthis |date=11 January 2022 |publisher=[[Voice of America]] |url=https://www.voanews.com/a/saudi-coalition-yemeni-allies-capture-energy-rich-shabwa-province-from-houthis/6392470.html }}</ref> This operation was supported and directed by the United Arab Emirates.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Johnsen |first=Gregory D. |title=The UAE's Three Strategic Interests in Yemen |date=24 February 2022 |publisher=The Arab Gulf States Institute |url=https://agsiw.org/the-uaes-three-strategic-interests-in-yemen/ }}</ref>

