ports Worsen Crisis (as of 23 June 2015) |publisher=UN Office for the Coordination of Humanitarian Affairs |date=23 June 2015 |url=http://reliefweb.int/report/yemen/yemen-reduced-imports-worsen-crisis-23-june-2015 |archive-url=https://www.webcitation.org/6ZWE5RPfG?url=http://reliefweb.int/report/yemen/yemen-reduced-imports-worsen-crisis-23-june-2015 |archive-date=24 June 2015 |url-status=live }}; PDF: {{cite web |title=Yemen: Reduced Imports Worsen Crisis (as of 23 June 2015) |url=http://reliefweb.int/sites/reliefweb.int/files/resources/Reduced%20imports%20worsen%20crisis%2023%20June%202015.pdf |archive-url=https://www.webcitation.org/6ZWEIEP87?url=http://reliefweb.int/sites/reliefweb.int/files/resources/Reduced%20imports%20worsen%20crisis%2023%20June%202015.pdf |archive-date=24 June 2015 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="UN-OCHA_2015-12-14_SOS">{{cite web |title=Yemen: Snapshot on Shipping and Food Imports (as of 14 December 2015) |publisher=UN Office for the Coordination of Humanitarian Affairs |date=14 December 2015 |url=http://reliefweb.int/report/yemen/yemen-snapshot-shipping-and-food-imports-14-december-2015 |archive-url=https://www.webcitation.org/6dmT2WH3T?url=http://reliefweb.int/report/yemen/yemen-snapshot-shipping-and-food-imports-14-december-2015 |archive-date=14 December 2015 |url-status=live }}; PDF: {{cite web |title=Yemen: Snapshot on Shipping and Food Imports (as of 14 December 2015 |url=http://reliefweb.int/sites/reliefweb.int/files/resources/OCHA_Yemen_Shipment_snapshot_November.pdf |archive-url=https://www.webcitation.org/6dmTXpuS7?url=http://reliefweb.int/sites/reliefweb.int/files/resources/OCHA_Yemen_Shipment_snapshot_November.pdf |archive-date=14 December 2015 |url-status=live }}</ref>
|content= {{Chart | chart = Yemen fuel imports 2015-2016.Bar.chart}} }}

Saudi Arabia faced growing criticism for the Saudi-led naval and air blockade, which effectively isolated the country.<ref name="NYT_2015-05-12_CFY">{{cite news |last1=Kalfood |first1=Mohammed Ali |last2=Fahim |first2=Kareem |title=A Cease-Fire in Yemen, but Fighting Is Persistent |newspaper=[[The New York Times]] |date=12 May 2015 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2015/05/13/world/middleeast/iranian-cargo-ship-heads-toward-yemen-and-saudi-blockade.html|archive-url=https://www.webcitation.org/6e8Q7sdIs?url=http://www.nytimes.com/2015/05/13/world/middleeast/iranian-cargo-ship-heads-toward-yemen-and-saudi-blockade.html?_r=1 |archive-date=29 December 2015 |url-status=live }} (Printed version: ''The New York Times'', 13 May 2015, page A10 of the New York edition).</ref>

A "military source and pro-Hadi militiamen" told the AFP on 26 April that coalition warships were participating in the shelling of Aden.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.yahoo.com/least-27-killed-south-yemen-fighting-102831816.html |title=Clashes rage in Yemen as calls for peace talks grow |date=26 April 2015 |work=[[Yahoo! News]] |agency=Agence France-Presse |access-date=13 September 2024 |archive-date=27 January 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190127204249/https://news.yahoo.com/least-27-killed-south-yemen-fighting-102831816.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

On 30 April, the Iranian navy announced it had deployed two destroyers to the Gulf of Aden to "ensure the safety of commercial ships of our country against the threat of pirates", according to a rear admiral.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.yahoo.com/iran-says-warships-entrance-key-yemen-strait-130223125.html |title=Iran says warships at entrance to key Yemen strait |date=30 April 2015 |work=[[Yahoo! News]] |agency=Agence France-Presse |access-date=13 September 2024 |archive-date=28 January 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190128213200/https://news.yahoo.com/iran-says-warships-entrance-key-yemen-strait-130223125.html |url-status=live }}</ref> According to the same source, the deployment was scheduled to last until mid-June. Iran's deputy foreign minister, [[Hossein Amir-Abdollahian]], told state-run [[Tasnim News Agency]], "others will not be allowed to put our shared security at risk with military adventures".<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/yemen-security-iran-idUSL5N0XT08L20150502|publisher=[[Reuters]]|title=Iran pledges to protect shared security interests with Yemen -Tasnim|date=2 May 2015|access-date=2 May 2015|archive-date=6 March 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160306230335/http://www.reuters.com/article/yemen-security-iran-idUSL5N0XT08L20150502|url-status=live}}</ref>

==== UN verification mechanism and the evolving blockade (2016–2022) ====

To address continued disruption of commercial shipping under the coalition blockade, the United Nations Office for Project Services (UNOPS) operationalized the UN Verification and Inspection Mechanism for Yemen (UNVIM) on 2 May 2016. Based in Djibouti, UNVIM was tasked with clearing cargo vessels bound for Houthi-held Red Sea ports under UN Security Council Resolution 2216.<ref name="UN-press-2016">{{cite web |title=Secretary-General Welcomes New United Nations Verification Mechanism in Djibouti to Facilitate Flow of Goods, Services into Yemen |publisher=United Nations |date=2 May 2016 |url=https://press.un.org/en/2016/sgsm17730.doc.htm |access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> By the end of 2016 the mechanism had cleared several hundred vessels carrying millions of tonnes of food, fuel and other supplies, though [[Human Rights Watch]] documented that the coalition continued to delay or divert fuel tankers even after UNVIM had granted clearance.<ref name="HRW-2017">{{cite web |title=Yemen: Coalition Blockade Imperils Civilians |publisher=Human Rights Watch |date=7 December 2017 |url=https://www.hrw.org/news/2017/12/07/yemen-coalition-blockade-imperils-civilians |access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> Restrictions tightened sharply in November 2017, when the coalition halted nearly all shipping to Houthi-controlled ports for roughly three weeks following a ballistic missile fired toward Riyadh, deepening fuel and food shortages before a partial reopening later that month.<ref name="HRW-2017" />

A planned coalition offensive against the port city of Hodeidah was averted when government and Houthi delegations reached the Stockholm Agreement on 13 December 2018, providing for a ceasefire and mutual redeployment of forces from Hodeidah and the smaller ports of Salif and Ras Isa.<ref name="CrisisGroup-Stockholm">{{cite web |title=Saving the Stockholm Agreement and Averting a Regional Conflagration in Yemen |publisher=International Crisis Group |date=14 August 2019 |url=https://www.crisisgroup.org/rpt/middle-east-north-africa/yemen/203-saving-stockholm-agreement-and-averting-regional-conflagration-yemen |access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> A UN mission (UNMHA) was deployed to monitor the agreement, and although full redeployment of forces was never completed, the ceasefire around the port largely held even as fighting continued on other fronts.<ref name="UN-Hodeidah-2019">{{cite web |title=Hodeidah Ceasefire Holding But Faster Progress Key to Stopping Yemen from Sliding into Regional War, Deepening Humanitarian Crisis, Speakers Tell Security Council |publisher=United Nations |date=18 July 2019 |url=https://www.un.org/press/en/2019/sc13887.doc.htm |access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref>

A more substantial easing followed the UN-brokered nationwide truce that took effect on 2 April 2022. Fuel tankers were thereafter permitted into Hodeidah largely without the delays in coalition "holding areas" that had previously been routine, and by early 2023 UNVIM data showed no fuel or bulk food vessel had been denied clearance to the port for almost a year.<ref name="RAND-truce">{{cite web |title=Yemen's Year-Long Truce Creates Opportunities for Durable Peace |publisher=RAND Corporation |date=10 July 2023 |url=https://www.rand.org/pubs/commentary/2023/07/yemens-year-long-truce-creates-opportunities-for-durable.html |access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> The truce formally lapsed on 2 October 2022 after the warring parties failed to agree on an extension, but neither side resumed cross-border drone or missile attacks, and the calm at sea persisted in practice into 2023.<ref name="UNNews-truceend">{{cite web |title=Yemen: End of nationwide truce heralds 'heightened risk of war', Security Council hears |publisher=UN News |date=13 October 2022 |url=https://news.un.org/en/story/2022/10/1129517 |access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref>

==== Houthi attacks on shipping and the Red Sea crisis (2023–2025) ====
{{Main|Red Sea crisis}}

The nature of naval involvement in the Yemen conflict was transformed by the Gaza war, which began in October 2023. As the Houthis began launching missiles and drones at Israel, several were intercepted by US and allied naval and air defences operating in the Red Sea.<ref name="CFR-tracker">{{cite web |title=Conflict in Yemen and the Red Sea |publisher=Council on Foreign Relations |work=Global Conflict Tracker |url=https://www.cfr.org/global-conflict-tracker/conflict/war-yemen |access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> On 19 November 2023, Houthi fighters used a helicopter to board and seize the car carrier ''Galaxy Leader'' in the southern Red Sea, detaining its 25 crew members; the Houthis cited the vessel's links to an Israeli businessman, while Western officials suggested an Iranian intelligence-gathering ship in the area may have helped identify the target.<ref name="National-GalaxyLeader">{{cite news |title=IRGC spy ship suspected of role in Houthis' capture of Galaxy Leader in Red Sea |publisher=The National |date=21 November 2023 |url=https://www.thenationalnews.com/mena/palestine-israel/2023/11/22/houthi-ship-hijack-red-sea/ |access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> The seizure opened a sustained campaign against shipping the group deemed Israeli-linked, which within weeks expanded to vessels connected to dozens of other nationalities.<ref name="CFR-tracker" />

The United States responded by announcing the multinational Operation Prosperity Guardian on 18 December 2023 to escort merchant shipping, eventually drawing participation from more than twenty countries.<ref name="Wilson-timeline">{{cite web |title=Timeline: Houthi Attacks |publisher=Wilson Center |url=https://www.wilsoncenter.org/article/timeline-houthi-attacks |access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> On 11–12 January 2024 the United States and United Kingdom, with backing from several allies, launched Operation Poseidon Archer, striking some 60 Houthi missile, radar and storage sites with more than 150 precision-guided munitions; further rounds of strikes followed through 2024 under the same operation.<ref name="Wilson-timeline" /> Days later the UN Security Council adopted a resolution condemning the attacks and affirming the right of states to defend commercial and naval vessels transiting the Red Sea.<ref name="SCR-2026">{{cite web |title=Yemen: Vote on a Draft Resolution Extending the Monitoring Requirement on Houthi Attacks in the Red Sea and Monthly Briefing and Consultations |publisher=Security Council Report |date=January 2026 |url=https://www.securitycouncilreport.org/whatsinblue/2026/01/yemen-vote-on-a-draft-resolution-extending-the-monitoring-requirement-on-houthi-attacks-in-the-red-sea-and-monthly-briefing-and-consultations.php |access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> On 19 February 2024 the European Union launched its own, explicitly defensive naval mission, EUNAVFOR Aspides, headquartered in Larissa, Greece, to escort merchant vessels and build maritime situational awareness without striking targets ashore.<ref name="EEAS-Aspides">{{cite web |title=About the Operation EUNAVFOR ASPIDES |publisher=European External Action Service |url=https://www.eeas.europa.eu/eunavfor-aspides/about-operation-eunavfor-aspides_en |access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref>

Despite this naval build-up, Houthi attacks continued and at times escalated. The bulk carrier ''Rubymar'' sank off the Yemeni coast in March 2024 after being struck, the first vessel lost in the campaign.<ref name="CrisisGroup-RedSea">{{cite web |title=Calming the Red Sea's Turbulent Waters |publisher=International Crisis Group |date=21 March 2025 |url=https://www.crisisgroup.org/rpt/middle-east-north-africa/gulf-and-arabian-peninsula/yemen/248-calming-red-seas-turbulent-waters |access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> The bulk carrier ''Tutor'' sank in June 2024 after an attack involving an uncrewed surface vessel and missiles that killed a crew member, then the most sophisticated strike of the campaign.<ref name="WashInstitute-Tutor">{{cite web |title=Houthi Ship Attacks Pose a Longer-Term Challenge to Regional Security and Trade Plans |publisher=The Washington Institute |date=26 June 2024 |url=https://www.washingtoninstitute.org/policy-analysis/houthi-ship-attacks-pose-longer-term-challenge-regional-security-and-trade-plans |access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> By October 2024 the United States estimated the Houthis had launched nearly 190 attacks on commercial and naval vessels since the campaign began, and container shipping capacity transiting the Red Sea was running roughly 64 percent below pre-crisis levels.<ref name="HoC-library">{{cite web |title=UK and international response to Houthis in the Red Sea 2024/25 |publisher=House of Commons Library |url=https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/cbp-9930/ |access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref>

A Gaza ceasefire in January 2025 briefly stilled the Houthi campaign,<ref name="CrisisGroup-RedSea" /> and on 22 January the group released the ''Galaxy Leader''{{'s}} crew after 14 months in captivity, in a handover mediated by Oman.<ref name="PBS-GalaxyRelease">{{cite news |title=Houthi rebels release crew of Galaxy Leader ship seized in Red Sea in November 2023 |publisher=PBS NewsHour |date=23 January 2025 |url=https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/houthi-rebels-release-crew-of-galaxy-leader-ship-seized-in-red-sea-in-november-2023 |access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> When that ceasefire later broke down, the United States launched an expanded campaign of strikes, Operation Rough Rider, from March to May 2025 in an effort to compel a lasting end to the attacks.<ref name="CFR-AnotherHormuz">{{cite news |title=Another Hormuz? The Red Sea's Threat to the Global Economy |publisher=Council on Foreign Relations |date=24 June 2026 |url=https://www.cfr.org/articles/another-hormuz-the-red-seas-threat-to-the-global-economy |access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> The campaign ended in May 2025 with an Oman-brokered agreement under which the Houthis agreed to stop targeting US vessels in exchange for a halt to US strikes, though the deal did not extend to Israeli-linked shipping.<ref name="CFR-AnotherHormuz" /> Attacks on other shipping resumed in July 2025: early that month the Houthis sank two further bulk carriers, the ''Magic Seas'' and the ''Eternity C'', killing four of the latter's crew and taking eleven others captive, before declaring a "fourth phase" of their campaign targeting any vessel whose operating company had dealings with Israeli ports, regardless of flag.<ref name="PBS-fourthphase">{{cite news |title=Houthis threaten to escalate attacks on ships linked to companies dealing with Israel |publisher=PBS NewsHour |date=28 July 2025 |url=https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/houthis-threaten-to-escalate-attacks-on-ships-linked-to-companies-dealing-with-israel |access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref>

The campaign wound down again after a further Gaza ceasefire took hold in October 2025; the Houthis suspended attacks on shipping and Israel,<ref name="CRS-USNI">{{cite news |title=Report to Congress on Yemen and Red Sea Security |publisher=USNI News |date=25 February 2026 |url=https://news.usni.org/2026/02/25/report-to-congress-on-yemen-and-red-sea-security |access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> and in November formally announced an end to the campaign and the lifting of their blockade on Israeli ports.<ref name="MaritimeExec">{{cite web |title=Houthis Announce End of Red Sea Shipping Attacks |publisher=The Maritime Executive |date=11 November 2025 |url=https://maritime-executive.com/article/houthis-announce-end-of-red-sea-shipping-attacks |access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref>

==== Renewed tensions amid the 2026 Iran war ====

By January 2026, the lull had allowed major carriers including Maersk and CMA CGM to resume scheduled Red Sea services, but the calm proved temporary.<ref name="FreightWaves">{{cite news |title=UPDATED: Iran-backed Houthi warn of new Red Sea attacks |publisher=FreightWaves |date=28 February 2026 |url=https://www.freightwaves.com/news/iran-backed-houthi-warn-of-new-red-sea-attacks |access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> After Israel and the United States launched strikes on Iran in late February 2026, opening the 2026 Iran war, the Houthis warned on 28 February that they would resume attacks on Israel and on Red Sea shipping.<ref name="FreightWaves" /> The group resumed direct strikes on Israel on 28 March 2026, and separately warned it might close the Bab-el-Mandeb strait if Gulf Arab states joined the war against Iran, raising fears of a second maritime chokepoint crisis alongside Iran's disruption of the Strait of Hormuz.<ref name="JPost-missiles">{{cite news |title=Houthis improving their missiles during current ceasefire, sources tell 'Post' |publisher=The Jerusalem Post |date=29 June 2026 |url=https://www.jpost.com/middle-east/article-900794 |access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref><ref name="FoxNews-chokepoint">{{cite news |title=Houthis threaten second Red Sea shipping chokepoint amid Iran conflict |publisher=Fox News |date=7 April 2026 |url=https://www.foxnews.com/politics/second-choke-point-crisis-looms-houthis-threaten-key-red-sea-shipping-lane |access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> In practice, the group limited itself to a small number of missile and drone strikes on Israel rather than a renewed large-scale maritime campaign, restraint that analysts linked to pressure from regional actors and concern over the group's domestic standing in Yemen.<ref name="FoxNews-chokepoint" />

Following a ceasefire between the United States, Israel and Iran, the Houthis kept up only sporadic strikes on Israel while reportedly using the lull to improve the range and accuracy of their missile arsenal, according to Western intelligence sources.<ref name="JPost-missiles" /> On 8 June 2026 the group announced a renewed, complete ban on Israeli-flagged or Israeli-linked vessels transiting the Red Sea, branding them "legitimate military targets", even as its wider campaign against non-Israeli shipping remained suspended.<ref name="CFR-AnotherHormuz" /> European and other coalition naval forces remained deployed in the area under extended mandates; the EU's Operation Aspides mission, for instance, was extended in February 2026 through February 2027.<ref name="Consilium-2026">{{cite web |title=Red Sea: Council extends the mandate of Operation ASPIDES to safeguard freedom of navigation |publisher=Council of the European Union |date=23 February 2026 |url=https://www.consilium.europa.eu/en/press/press-releases/2026/02/23/red-sea-council-extends-the-mandate-of-operation-aspides-to-safeguard-freedom-of-navigation/ |access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> As of late June 2026, with the Strait of Hormuz only slowly reopening under its own ceasefire, the prospect of renewed large-scale Houthi attacks on the Red Sea continued to weigh on global shipping and energy markets.<ref name="CFR-AnotherHormuz" />

=== Scale and participation of Saudi-led coalition members ===
Pakistan was called on by Saudi Arabia to join the coalition, but its parliament voted to maintain neutrality.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.geo.tv/article-180993-Parliament-passes-resolution-for-neutrality-in-Yemen-conflict|title=Parliament passes resolution for neutrality in Yemen conflict – PAKISTAN – geo.tv|date=10 April 2015|access-date=15 April 2015|archive-date=24 April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150424004443/http://www.geo.tv/article-180993-Parliament-passes-resolution-for-neutrality-in-Yemen-conflict|url-status=live}}</ref> In February 2016, [[Constellis]] (commonly known as Blackwater, named Academi at the time) withdrew from front-line duties in the Yemen campaign.<ref name="arwa1"/> Qatar was suspended from the coalition due to the [[2017 Qatar diplomatic crisis]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.business-standard.com/article/international/gulf-rift-deepens-saudi-suspends-qatar-troops-involvement-in-yemen-war-117060500280_1.html|title=Gulf rift deepens: Saudi suspends Qatar troops' involvement in Yemen war|agency=ANI|date=5 June 2017|newspaper=Business Standard|access-date=30 October 2017|archive-date=13 September 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240913004709/https://www.business-standard.com/article/international/gulf-rift-deepens-saudi-suspends-qatar-troops-involvement-in-yemen-war-117060500280_1.html|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Morocco]] ended their participation in 2019 due to deterioration of [[Morocco–Saudi Arabia relations]]<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.news24.com/Africa/News/morocco-suspends-participation-in-saudi-led-war-in-yemen-20190208|title=Morocco suspends participation in Saudi-led war in Yemen|agency=Al Jazeera|date=8 February 2019|website=News24|access-date=1 August 2019|archive-date=5 September 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190905182923/https://www.news24.com/Africa/News/morocco-suspends-participation-in-saudi-led-war-in-yemen-20190208|url-status=live}}</ref> followed by [[United Arab Emirates]] in July 2019 amid possible tensions with Iran on the Persian Gulf and differences with Saudi Arabia.<ref name="auto1"/> Sudan announced its decision to reduce troops commitment from 15,000 to 5,000 in early December 2019.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-sudan-politics-idUKKBN1YC0HX|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191208223342/https://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-sudan-politics-idUKKBN1YC0HX|archive-date=8 December 2019|title=Sudan says it has reduced troops in Yemen to 5,000|publisher=Reuters|date=8 December 2019|via=uk.reuters.com}}</ref>

Saudi Arabia had 2,500 troops deployed for Operation Restoring Hope as of 2024, a total of two armored brigades from the [[Royal Saudi Land Forces]], according to the [[International Institute for Strategic Studies]].<ref name="IISS2024" />

